window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-bio-food-production","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production · Practice","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"To isolate naked protoplasts from plant cells, the cell wall is digested using:","image":"","options":["Cellulase and pectinase","Chitinase and lysozyme","Amylase and lipase","Protease and ligase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The plant cell wall is made mainly of cellulose, and pectin holds neighbouring cells together.\nCellulase breaks down cellulose and pectinase breaks down pectin. Together they release protoplasts that are bounded only by the plasma membrane.\nChitinase acts on fungal walls and lysozyme acts on bacterial walls, so they are not suitable here.\nAmylase, lipase, protease and ligase do not digest plant cell walls.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about dairy farm management are correct?\n(i) Regular visits by a veterinary doctor are mandatory.\n(ii) Strict hygiene is needed for the cattle, but the hygiene of handlers does not affect milk quality.\n(iii) Mechanised milking reduces the chance of direct contact of the produce with the handler.\n(iv) Only the quantity of fodder matters; its quality has little effect on yield.\n(v) Proper record keeping helps detect problems early.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT says regular veterinary visits are mandatory.\n(ii) Incorrect: cleanliness of both cattle and handlers is of paramount importance.\n(iii) Correct: mechanisation reduces direct contact of the produce with the handler.\n(iv) Incorrect: both quality and quantity of fodder are emphasised.\n(v) Correct: inspections with records help identify problems early.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the steps of conventional breeding for disease resistance in the correct order:\nP. Hybridisation of selected parents\nQ. Testing and release of new varieties\nR. Screening germplasm for resistance sources\nS. Selection and evaluation of the hybrids","image":"","options":["P → R → S → Q","R → S → P → Q","R → P → S → Q","S → R → P → Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First, the germplasm is screened to find sources of resistance (R).\nThe resistant parents are then hybridised (P).\nNext, the hybrids are selected and evaluated (S).\nFinally, the best lines are tested and released as new varieties (Q).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Bee-keeping can be practised only in areas with large commercial fruit orchards.\nStatement II: Beehives can be kept in a courtyard, on the verandah of a house or even on the roof.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Bee-keeping can be practised wherever there are sufficient bee pastures. These may be wild shrubs, fruit orchards or cultivated crops, so large orchards are not essential.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT says hives can be kept in a courtyard, on a verandah or on the roof.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hybrids selected as superior to both parents are self-pollinated for several generations until they reach a state of uniformity (homozygosity).\nStatement II: A newly bred cultivar is released once it performs well in research fields under ideal irrigation and fertiliser conditions; trials in farmers' fields are not needed.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Selected superior hybrids are selfed for several generations to make them homozygous, so their characters do not segregate in the progeny.\nStatement II is incorrect. After research-field evaluation, the cultivar must still be tested in farmers' fields for at least three growing seasons, at several locations in different agroclimatic zones, before it is released.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Animal husbandry is the agricultural practice of breeding and raising livestock. In its extended sense, which of the following is also included in animal husbandry?","image":"","options":["Mushroom cultivation","Poultry farming and fisheries","Micropropagation of banana","Biofortification of cereals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Livestock includes buffaloes, cows, pigs, horses, sheep, camels and goats.\nIn its extended sense, NCERT includes poultry farming and fisheries within animal husbandry.\nMushrooms are fungi, so their cultivation is not animal husbandry.\nMicropropagation and biofortification are plant-improvement techniques.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) $Apis\\ indica$\n(b) Hilsa\n(c) Rohu\n(d) Edible oyster\nList-II\n(i) Freshwater fish\n(ii) Marine fish\n(iii) Aquatic shellfish used as food\n(iv) Most common honeybee species reared","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $Apis\\ indica$ is the most common honeybee species reared, so it matches (iv).\n(b) Hilsa is listed by NCERT as a marine fish, so it matches (ii).\n(c) Rohu is a freshwater fish, so it matches (i).\n(d) The edible oyster is a mollusc, a shellfish used as food rather than a fish, so it matches (iii).\nOption (2) is the trap: it swaps Hilsa and Rohu.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II in the context of MOET:\nList-I\n(A) Superovulation\n(B) Elite bull\n(C) Surrogate mother\n(D) Genetic mother after embryo recovery\nList-II\n(i) Carries the transferred embryo to term\n(ii) Release of 6-8 eggs in one cycle\n(iii) Available for another round of superovulation\n(iv) Used for mating, or as a semen source for insemination","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Superovulation produces 6-8 eggs instead of one, so A matches (ii).\n(B) The superovulated cow is either mated with an elite bull or artificially inseminated, so B matches (iv).\n(C) The surrogate mother receives the 8-32 cell embryo and carries the pregnancy, so C matches (i).\n(D) After the embryos are recovered, the genetic mother can be superovulated again, so D matches (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Spirulina is promoted as a source of single cell protein (SCP). Spirulina is a:","image":"","options":["Cyanobacterium (blue-green alga)","Unicellular yeast","Protozoan","Methane-utilising bacterium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Spirulina is a filamentous cyanobacterium, also called a blue-green alga.\nIt is grown on a large scale as a protein-rich food, so it counts as a single cell protein source.\nIt is not a fungus like yeast, and it is not a protozoan.\nThe bacterium named in NCERT for its very high protein yield is Methylophilus methylotrophus, not Spirulina.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Breeding for improved nutritional quality aims at all of the following EXCEPT improving:","image":"","options":["Protein content and quality","Oil content and quality","Micronutrient and mineral content","Fibre and cellulose content"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists four objectives of breeding for improved nutritional quality.\nThese are protein content and quality, oil content and quality, vitamin content, and micronutrient and mineral content.\nFibre and cellulose content is not one of these objectives.\nOptions 1 to 3 all appear in the NCERT list.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about inbreeding and out-crossing are correct?\n(i) Continued close inbreeding usually reduces fertility and even productivity.\n(ii) Inbreeding depression can be countered by mating selected animals with unrelated superior animals of the same breed.\n(iii) A single out-cross often helps to overcome inbreeding depression.\n(iv) Inbreeding depression arises because inbreeding increases heterozygosity.\n(v) Out-crossing is the best breeding method for animals that are below average in productivity.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this fall in fertility and productivity is called inbreeding depression.\n(ii) Correct: mating with unrelated superior animals of the same breed usually restores fertility and yield.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT notes that a single out-cross often overcomes inbreeding depression.\n(iv) Incorrect: inbreeding increases homozygosity, not heterozygosity.\n(v) Correct: out-crossing suits animals below average in milk yield, growth rate and similar traits.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although India and China hold more than 70% of the world's livestock, their contribution to world farm produce is only about 25%.\nReason (R): The productivity per unit of livestock in these countries is very low.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT gives exactly these figures: more than 70% of the world's livestock, but only about 25% of world farm produce.\nR is true. NCERT itself concludes that productivity per unit is very low.\nR explains A: a huge number of animals gives a small share of produce only because each animal yields little.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Norman E. Borlaug\n(b) IR-8\n(c) Taichung Native-1\n(d) Sonalika\nList-II\n(i) Semi-dwarf rice from Taiwan\n(ii) Semi-dwarf wheat introduced in India in 1963\n(iii) Semi-dwarf wheat developed in Mexico\n(iv) Semi-dwarf rice from IRRI, Philippines","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Borlaug developed semi-dwarf wheat at the International Centre for Wheat and Maize Improvement in Mexico, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) IR-8 came from IRRI, Philippines, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) Taichung Native-1 came from Taiwan, so (c)-(i).\n(d) Sonalika was one of the high-yielding, disease-resistant wheat varieties introduced in 1963, so (d)-(ii).\nOption (3) swaps the two rice sources.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Parbhani Kranti, a variety of bhindi (Abelmoschus esculentus), is resistant to yellow mosaic virus. How was this resistance obtained?","image":"","options":["By inducing mutations with gamma radiation","By transferring a resistance gene from a wild species","By somatic hybridisation with tomato protoplasts","By meristem culture to obtain virus-free plants"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Resistance to yellow mosaic virus in bhindi came from a wild relative. The gene was transferred to the cultivated species, giving the variety Parbhani Kranti.\nMutation breeding (option 1) is NCERT's example for mung bean, not bhindi.\nMeristem culture only gives virus-free plants. It does not make a variety genetically resistant.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Saccharum barberi, originally grown in north India, had poor sugar content and yield. Saccharum officinarum of south India had thicker stems and higher sugar but did not grow well in north India; the two were crossed to combine their desirable traits.\nStatement II: High-yielding semi-dwarf rice varieties were first derived from IR-8 (developed at IRRI, Philippines) and Taichung Native-1 (from Taiwan); later, better-yielding semi-dwarf varieties Jaya and Ratna were developed in India.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the cross combined the high yield, thick stems and high sugar of S. officinarum with the ability of S. barberi to grow in north India.\nStatement II is correct: the semi-dwarf rice varieties were derived from IR-8 and Taichung Native-1 and introduced in 1966. Jaya and Ratna were later developed in India.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Explant\n(B) Somaclone\n(C) Protoplast\n(D) Pomato\nList-II\n(I) Plant cell bounded only by plasma membrane\n(II) Somatic hybrid of tomato and potato\n(III) Plant part grown in vitro in a nutrient medium\n(IV) Plant genetically identical to its source plant","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Explant: any plant part taken out and grown under sterile conditions in a nutrient medium, so A-III.\n(B) Somaclone: plant from micropropagation that is genetically identical to its parent, so B-IV.\n(C) Protoplast: naked cell left after wall digestion, so C-I.\n(D) Pomato: product of fusing tomato and potato protoplasts, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following vegetables were released by IARI as vitamin C enriched varieties?\nBitter gourd, Mustard, Tomato, Carrot, Pumpkin, French bean, Bathua","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Vitamin C enriched: bitter gourd, mustard, tomato and bathua, which makes 4.\nCarrot and pumpkin were released for vitamin A.\nFrench bean was released for protein.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are biochemical (not morphological) bases of insect resistance described for crop plants?\n(i) Hairy leaves in cotton\n(ii) Solid stems in wheat\n(iii) High aspartic acid in maize\n(iv) Low nitrogen content in maize\n(v) Hairy leaves in wheat\n(vi) Low sugar content in maize","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Hairy leaves in cotton: morphological (resistance to jassids).\n(ii) Solid stems in wheat: morphological (resistance to stem sawfly).\n(v) Hairy leaves in wheat: morphological (resistance to cereal leaf beetle).\n(iii), (iv) and (vi): high aspartic acid, low nitrogen and low sugar in maize are all biochemical. Together they give resistance to stem borers.\nSo three of the six are biochemical.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Explant\n(b) Protoplast\n(c) Somaclone\n(d) Pomato\nList-II\n(i) Plant cell whose wall has been removed\n(ii) Somatic hybrid of two different plants\n(iii) Plant part grown in a nutrient medium under sterile conditions\n(iv) Plant genetically identical to the plant from which it was cultured\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) An explant is any plant part taken out and grown in vitro under sterile conditions, so it matches (iii).\n(b) A protoplast is a naked cell bounded only by the plasma membrane, so it matches (i).\n(c) A somaclone is genetically identical to its source plant, so it matches (iv).\n(d) Pomato is a somatic hybrid of tomato and potato, so it matches (ii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In a cattle-breeding programme, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Inbreeding helps accumulate superior genes and eliminate less desirable genes","A superior bull for inbreeding is selected because the bull himself shows the highest milk-related traits, without reference to his offspring","Hybrid progeny from cross-breeding may be subjected to inbreeding and selection to develop new stable breeds","Out-crossing is the best breeding method for animals that are below average in productivity, such as low milk yield"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Option (1) is correct: NCERT states that inbreeding accumulates superior genes and removes less desirable ones.\nOption (2) is incorrect: a bull does not produce milk himself. A superior male is one that gives rise to superior progeny, so he is judged by his offspring.\nOption (3) is correct: hybrids from cross-breeding can be inbred and selected to form new, stable breeds.\nOption (4) is correct: NCERT describes out-crossing as the best method for below-average animals.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In an inbreeding programme for cattle, superior males and superior females are identified and mated in pairs. On what basis is a cow identified as a 'superior female' and a bull as a 'superior male'?","image":"","options":["Cow: largest body size; Bull: greatest body weight","Cow: produces more milk per lactation; Bull: gives rise to superior progeny compared with other males","Cow: produces the most calves in a lifetime; Bull: has no common ancestor with the cow","Cow: belongs to a different breed from the bull; Bull: has the highest growth rate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the criteria for cattle as follows.\nA superior female produces more milk per lactation.\nA superior male (bull) gives rise to superior progeny compared with other males, so it is judged by the quality of its offspring.\nOption 3 fails because having no common ancestor describes out-crossing, not inbreeding.\nOption 4 fails because parents from different breeds describe cross-breeding.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Poultry is the class of domesticated fowl used for food or for their eggs.\nStatement II: Since poultry typically means chicken and ducks, turkey and geese can never be considered poultry.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: this is how NCERT defines poultry.\nStatement II is incorrect: poultry typically includes chicken and ducks, and sometimes also turkey and geese.\nIn a more general sense, the term may even refer to the meat of other birds.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A cow normally yields one egg per oestrous cycle. After superovulation treatment in a MOET programme, about how many eggs does she produce per cycle?","image":"","options":["2-3","4-5","6-8","15-20"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Without treatment, a cow normally releases one egg per cycle.\nHormones with FSH-like activity cause superovulation, so she releases 6-8 eggs instead of one.\nThe other ranges do not match the NCERT figure.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"During a bird flu outbreak, a person eats chicken and eggs that have been thoroughly cooked above 100°C. The chance of catching bird flu from this food is:","image":"","options":["Very high","High","Moderate","Negligible"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The H5N1 virus does not survive thorough cooking.\nWhen chicken and eggs are properly cooked above 100°C, the virus is inactivated.\nThe risk of infection from such food is therefore negligible.\nThe real risk comes from handling infected live birds or raw products, not from properly cooked food.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Multiple Ovulation Embryo Transfer Technology (MOET) is incorrect?","image":"","options":["A cow is given hormones with FSH-like activity to induce follicular maturation and superovulation","About 6-8 eggs are produced per cycle instead of the usual one egg","Fertilised eggs at the 8-32 cell stage are recovered surgically and transferred to surrogate mothers","The technique has been applied to cattle, sheep, rabbits, buffaloes and mares"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: FSH-like hormones induce follicular maturation and superovulation.\n(2) Correct: about 6-8 eggs are produced per cycle instead of one.\n(3) Incorrect: embryos at the 8-32 cell stage are recovered non-surgically, not surgically.\n(4) Correct: NCERT lists cattle, sheep, rabbits, buffaloes and mares.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Pusa Komal is a disease-resistant variety of:","image":"","options":["Cauliflower, resistant to black rot","Cowpea, resistant to bacterial blight","Brassica, resistant to white rust","Wheat, resistant to hill bunt"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In the NCERT table of resistant varieties, Pusa Komal is the cowpea variety resistant to bacterial blight.\nThe cauliflower varieties are Pusa Shubhra and Pusa Snowball K-1, resistant to black rot.\nThe Brassica variety resistant to white rust is Pusa Swarnim.\nThe wheat variety resistant to hill bunt is Himgiri.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the Multiple Ovulation Embryo Transfer (MOET) technology is correct?","image":"","options":["The donor cow is given progesterone so that it releases a single large egg each cycle","A hormone with FSH-like activity induces the cow to produce 6–8 eggs per cycle instead of one","Embryos are transferred to surrogate mothers only after they reach the 64–128 cell stage","The technique has been demonstrated only in cattle and cannot be used in other animals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In MOET, the donor cow is given a hormone with FSH-like activity. This induces superovulation, so it produces 6–8 eggs per cycle instead of one. The keyed statement is therefore correct.\nThe eggs are fertilised by mating or artificial insemination.\nEmbryos are recovered at the 8–32 cell stage, not 64–128 cells, and transferred to surrogate mothers.\nMOET has been demonstrated in cattle, sheep, rabbits, buffaloes and mares, so it is not limited to cattle.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Hisardale, a new breed of sheep developed in Punjab, was obtained by crossing:","image":"","options":["Marino ewes and Bikaneri rams","Bikaneri ewes and Marino rams","Bikaneri ewes and Bikaneri rams with no common ancestors","Female sheep and male goats"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hisardale was developed in Punjab by crossing Bikaneri ewes (females) with Marino rams (males).\nBikaneri and Marino are two breeds of the same species, so this is cross-breeding.\nOption 1 reverses the sexes of the parents, so it is wrong.\nMating Bikaneri animals with no common ancestors would be out-crossing, which does not produce a new breed.\nA sheep × goat mating would be interspecific hybridisation, not the method used for Hisardale.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about breeding for improved food quality are correct?\n(i) Maize hybrids developed in 2000 contained twice the amount of lysine and tryptophan compared to existing maize hybrids.\n(ii) 'Hidden hunger' refers to deficiencies of micronutrients, protein and vitamins in people who cannot afford enough fruits, vegetables, legumes, fish and meat.\n(iii) Pumpkin released by IARI is a vitamin C enriched vegetable.\n(iv) Garden peas are among the protein-enriched varieties released by IARI.\n(v) Biofortification aims only at raising the grain yield of cereals.","image":"","options":["Three","Two","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: these maize hybrids had twice the lysine and tryptophan of the existing hybrids.\n(ii) Correct: about three billion people suffer from this 'hidden hunger'.\n(iii) Incorrect: pumpkin, like carrot and spinach, was enriched in vitamin A. The vitamin C enriched group is bitter gourd, bathua, mustard and tomato.\n(iv) Correct: garden peas are listed with broad, lablab and French beans as protein enriched.\n(v) Incorrect: biofortification targets nutritional quality, namely protein, oil, vitamin and micronutrient content, not yield alone.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements regarding animal husbandry and dairy farm management are correct?\n(i) Feeding should be done scientifically, paying attention to both the quality and the quantity of fodder.\n(ii) Hygiene matters only for the cattle; the cleanliness of the milking handlers does not affect milk quality.\n(iii) Stringent cleanliness is required during milking, storage and transport of milk.\n(iv) Regular visits by a veterinarian are mandatory on a dairy farm.\n(v) Regular inspections with proper record keeping help to identify and rectify problems quickly.\n(vi) Animal husbandry, as described in NCERT, also includes poultry farming and fisheries.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: cattle must be fed scientifically, with attention to both the quality and the quantity of fodder.\n(ii) Incorrect: stringent hygiene is needed for both the cattle and the handlers.\n(iii) Correct: cleanliness is essential during milking, storage and transport.\n(iv) Correct: regular visits by a veterinarian are mandatory.\n(v) Correct: regular inspections and records help detect and rectify problems.\n(vi) Correct: NCERT includes poultry farming and fisheries under animal husbandry.\nTotal correct = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about fisheries is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Fishery covers the catching, processing and selling of fish, shellfish and other aquatic animals.","Fisheries provide income and employment to millions, especially in coastal states, and are the only livelihood for many people.","Pisciculture is a broader term than aquaculture and includes the culture of aquatic plants, prawns and oysters.","Aquaculture and pisciculture have increased the production of both freshwater and marine aquatic organisms."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this is NCERT's definition of fishery.\n(2) Correct: NCERT stresses employment in coastal states and that fishing is the sole livelihood for many.\n(3) Incorrect: pisciculture is the culture of fish only. Aquaculture is the broader term covering all useful aquatic organisms, including plants, prawns and oysters. The statement reverses the two terms.\n(4) Correct: these practices raised both freshwater and marine production.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In a plant tissue culture medium, which of the following is commonly supplied as the carbon source?","image":"","options":["Starch","Cellulose","Sucrose","Glycerol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Explants grown in vitro cannot depend fully on photosynthesis.\nSo the medium must contain a carbon source, and NCERT names sucrose.\nThe medium also contains inorganic salts, vitamins, amino acids and growth regulators such as auxins and cytokinins.\nStarch and cellulose are complex polysaccharides that the cultured tissue cannot readily use.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A single out-cross often helps to overcome inbreeding depression.\nReason (R): Out-crossing involves mating animals belonging to two different breeds.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Mating selected animals with unrelated superior animals usually restores fertility and yield. NCERT notes that a single out-cross often overcomes inbreeding depression.\nR is false. Out-crossing is mating within the same breed between animals that share no common ancestors for 4–6 generations.\nMating animals of two different breeds is cross-breeding, not out-crossing.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In 2000, maize hybrids were developed that contained twice the amount of which amino acids compared with existing maize hybrids?","image":"","options":["Lysine and tryptophan","Methionine and cysteine","Glycine and alanine","Lysine and glutamine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In 2000, new maize hybrids were developed with double the lysine and tryptophan of earlier hybrids.\nBoth are essential amino acids, so this improved the quality of maize protein.\nOption 4 is tempting because it includes lysine, but glutamine is not the second amino acid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"During the testing stage of a new crop cultivar, its performance in farmers' fields is compared with the best locally available cultivar of that crop. This local cultivar is called the:","image":"","options":["Parental line","Pure line","Check or reference cultivar","Germplasm accession"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In the final step of breeding, the new cultivar is grown in farmers' fields and compared with the best available local cultivar.\nNCERT calls this local cultivar the check or reference cultivar.\nA parental line is one of the plants used in the cross, so it is not the yardstick used in the field trials.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Plants produced by micropropagation that are genetically identical to the original plant from which they were grown are called:","image":"","options":["Somatic hybrids","Transgenics","Haploids","Somaclones"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Micropropagation produces thousands of plants in a short time.\nAll of these plants come from somatic tissue of one parent, so they are genetically identical to it.\nSuch plants are called somaclones.\nSomatic hybrids arise from the fusion of protoplasts of two different plants, so they are not identical to either parent.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match the crop variety in List-I with the disease it resists in List-II.\nList-I\nA. Himgiri (wheat)\nB. Pusa Swarnim (Brassica)\nC. Pusa Komal (cowpea)\nD. Pusa Sadabahar (chilli)\nList-II\n(i) Bacterial blight\n(ii) Chilly mosaic virus, tobacco mosaic virus and leaf curl\n(iii) Leaf and stripe rust, hill bunt\n(iv) White rust","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Himgiri wheat resists leaf and stripe rust and hill bunt, so A-(iii).\nB. Pusa Swarnim (Karan rai) resists white rust, so B-(iv).\nC. Pusa Komal cowpea resists bacterial blight, so C-(i).\nD. Pusa Sadabahar chilli resists chilly mosaic virus, TMV and leaf curl, so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match the disease-resistant crop varieties in List-I with the diseases they resist in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Himgiri (wheat)\n(b) Pusa Komal (cowpea)\n(c) Pusa Sadabahar (chilli)\n(d) Pusa Shubhra (cauliflower)\nList-II\n(i) Bacterial blight\n(ii) Black rot and curl blight black rot\n(iii) Leaf and stripe rust, hill bunt\n(iv) Chilli mosaic virus, Tobacco mosaic virus and leaf curl","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Himgiri wheat resists leaf and stripe rust and hill bunt, so (a) matches (iii).\n(b) Pusa Komal cowpea resists bacterial blight, so (b) matches (i).\n(c) Pusa Sadabahar chilli resists chilli mosaic virus, TMV and leaf curl, so (c) matches (iv).\n(d) Pusa Shubhra cauliflower resists black rot and curl blight black rot, so (d) matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In a cross between two bisexual-flowered plants, the flower of the female parent is emasculated and then covered with a bag. The main purpose of bagging is to:","image":"","options":["Speed up the maturation of the stigma","Protect the stigma from contamination by unwanted pollen","Stop the anthers of the same flower from shedding pollen","Increase humidity so that the pollen germinates"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Emasculation means removing the anthers before they dehisce, which prevents self-pollination.\nBagging then shuts out stray pollen. As a result, only pollen from the chosen male parent reaches the stigma.\nOption 3 is wrong because the anthers have already been removed during emasculation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Atlas 66, used in breeding programmes as a donor parent to improve cultivated wheat, is valued mainly for its:","image":"","options":["High protein content","High lysine and tryptophan content in the endosperm","Resistance to leaf and stripe rust","Fivefold higher iron content in the grain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the wheat variety Atlas 66 has a high protein content.\nIt has therefore been used as a donor for improving the protein content of cultivated wheat.\nTwice the amount of lysine and tryptophan is a feature of maize hybrids developed in 2000, not of Atlas 66.\nOver five times more iron is a feature of iron-fortified rice.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A dairy owner has one elite, high milk-yielding cow and wants to rapidly build a herd carrying her genes. Why would MOET achieve this faster than natural breeding of the same cow?","image":"","options":["MOET raises the cow's milk yield directly through hormone injections, so fewer cows are needed","Superovulation gives many embryos per cycle; surrogates carry them while the donor can be superovulated again","MOET produces genetically identical clones of the elite cow from her somatic cells","MOET lets the elite cow carry 6-8 calves at once in her own uterus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Superovulation gives 6-8 eggs per cycle instead of one. After mating or AI, the resulting embryos are moved to surrogate mothers.\nThe genetic mother does not have to carry the pregnancies, so she is soon free for another round of superovulation. Herd size therefore rises in a short time.\nOption 3 is wrong: the embryos come from fertilisation, so they are not somatic clones.\nOption 4 is wrong: the embryos develop in surrogates.\nOption 1 is wrong: the hormone is used to induce ovulation, not to increase milk yield.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Continued close inbreeding in cattle usually lowers their fertility and productivity.\nReason (R): Inbreeding increases heterozygosity, which brings harmful recessive genes together.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: continued inbreeding causes inbreeding depression, which reduces fertility and productivity.\nR is false: inbreeding increases homozygosity, not heterozygosity.\nIt is homozygosity that exposes harmful recessive genes, which are then eliminated by selection.\nInbreeding depression can be overcome by a single out-cross.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Pusa Gaurav\n(B) Pusa Sem 3\n(C) Pusa A-4\n(D) Smooth-leaved, nectar-less cotton\nList-II\n(i) Shoot and fruit borer\n(ii) Bollworms\n(iii) Aphids\n(iv) Jassids, aphids and fruit borer","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Pusa Gaurav is a Brassica variety resistant to aphids, so A matches (iii).\n(B) Pusa Sem 3 is a flat bean variety resistant to jassids, aphids and fruit borer, so B matches (iv).\n(C) Pusa A-4 is an okra variety resistant to shoot and fruit borer, so C matches (i).\n(D) Smooth-leaved, nectar-less cotton does not attract bollworms, so D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In dairy farm management, NCERT says the first step towards improving milk yield is choosing good breeds. What should these breeds combine?","image":"","options":["High-yielding potential under the local climatic conditions, together with resistance to diseases","Small body size and low fodder requirement, regardless of yield","Maximum milk yield alone, irrespective of climate or disease susceptibility","Disease resistance alone, since yield depends only on feeding"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT: milk yield depends mainly on the quality of breeds in the farm.\nGood breeds should have high-yielding potential under the climatic conditions of the area. They should also be resistant to diseases.\nOption (3) is the most tempting wrong choice. A high-yielding breed that is unsuited to the climate, or that falls ill easily, cannot keep up its yield.\nOption (4) is wrong because yield depends mainly on the breed. Feeding only supports it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\n(i) Single cell protein refers to protein-rich microbial biomass grown on an industrial scale for use as food or feed.\n(ii) More than 25 per cent of the human population suffers from hunger and malnutrition.\n(iii) Spirulina needs costly purified media and cannot be grown on agricultural or industrial wastes.\n(iv) Methylophilus methylotrophus is a highly efficient protein source because of its high rate of biomass production and growth.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(i) and (iii) only","(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. SCP is microbial biomass grown on an industrial scale as a source of good protein.\n(ii) Correct. NCERT states that more than 25% of humans face hunger and malnutrition.\n(iii) Incorrect. Spirulina grows easily on straw, molasses, potato-processing waste water, manure and sewage.\n(iv) Correct. NCERT attributes its huge yield to a high rate of biomass production and growth.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hilsa, sardines, mackerel and pomfrets are among the commercially important marine fishes.\nStatement II: Catla, Rohu and common carp are commercially important marine fishes.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: hilsa, sardines, mackerel and pomfrets are marine food fishes.\nStatement II is incorrect: Catla, Rohu and common carp are freshwater fishes, not marine fishes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mung bean plants resistant to yellow mosaic virus and powdery mildew were obtained without crossing with any wild relative.\nReason (R): In mutation breeding, genetic variations are induced with chemicals or gamma radiation, and plants showing the desired resistance are selected and used as a source in breeding.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that resistance to yellow mosaic virus and powdery mildew in mung bean was induced by mutations.\nR is true. Mutation breeding changes base sequences using chemicals or radiation such as gamma rays. Resistant plants are then selected, multiplied or used in breeding.\nR explains A, because the mung bean resistance came from induced mutation, not from a wild relative.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which feature of wheat plants leads to non-preference by the wheat stem sawfly?","image":"","options":["Hairy leaves","Solid stems","High sugar content","Nectar-less flowers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that wheat plants with solid stems are not preferred by the stem sawfly. This is a morphological basis of resistance.\nHairy leaves in wheat protect against the cereal leaf beetle, not the sawfly.\nHigh sugar content does not give resistance. In maize, it is low sugar that is linked to stem borer resistance.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Keeping beehives in or near crop fields can benefit a farmer even if he does not sell any honey.\nReason (R): Beeswax obtained from beehives is used in the preparation of cosmetics and polishes of various kinds.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Bees pollinate crops such as sunflower, Brassica, apple and pear. Hives kept in fields during flowering raise pollination efficiency and improve crop yield, so the farmer gains even without selling honey.\nR is true. Beeswax is an apiculture product used in cosmetics and polishes.\nR does not explain A. The benefit described in A comes from pollination, not from selling beeswax, which is another hive product.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A poultry farmer mated closely related birds of the same breed for many generations to obtain a pure line. Later, the flock showed falling fertility and egg productivity. Which explanation and remedy are both correct?","image":"","options":["Outbreeding depression; mate birds of two different species","Increased heterozygosity; continue mating close relatives","Viral infection only; cull the entire flock","Inbreeding depression; mate selected birds with unrelated superior birds of the same breed (out-crossing)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Continued inbreeding increases homozygosity, so harmful recessive alleles get expressed.\nThis causes inbreeding depression: reduced fertility and productivity.\nThe remedy is to mate selected birds with unrelated superior birds of the same breed. This out-crossing restores fertility and yield.\nOption (2) is wrong because inbreeding decreases heterozygosity, not increases it.\nOption (3) is wrong because the symptoms are explained by inbreeding, so culling is not the fix.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Hisardale, a new breed of sheep developed in Punjab, was obtained by crossing:","image":"","options":["Marino ewes with Bikaneri rams","Bikaneri ewes with Marino rams","A female horse with a male donkey","Bikaneri ewes with unrelated Bikaneri rams for 4-6 generations"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hisardale is NCERT's example of cross-breeding. In cross-breeding, superior males of one breed are mated with superior females of another breed.\nHere the females were Bikaneri ewes and the males were Marino rams.\nOption (1) reverses the sexes.\nOption (3) describes the mule, which is an interspecific hybrid.\nOption (4) describes out-crossing within a single breed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"The mule is produced by mating:","image":"","options":["A male horse with a female donkey; it is an example of cross-breeding","A male donkey with a female horse; it is an example of out-crossing","A male donkey with a female horse; it is an example of interspecific hybridisation","A male horse with a female donkey; it is an example of inbreeding"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In interspecific hybridisation, the male and female animals belong to two different related species.\nNCERT's example is the mule, obtained by mating a male donkey with a female horse.\nCross-breeding and out-crossing both involve animals of the same species. Inbreeding involves closely related animals of the same breed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Mating of animals within the same breed that have no common ancestors on either side of their pedigree for 4–6 generations is known as:","image":"","options":["Inbreeding","Cross-breeding","Out-crossing","Interspecific hybridisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Out-crossing is mating within the same breed between animals with no common ancestors on either side of the pedigree for 4–6 generations. The offspring is called an out-cross.\nInbreeding is the opposite: closely related animals of the same breed are mated.\nCross-breeding involves two different breeds.\nInterspecific hybridisation involves two different species.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"The ability of a single plant cell or explant to regenerate into a complete plant is termed:","image":"","options":["Differentiation","Totipotency","Somatic hybridisation","Emasculation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines totipotency as the capacity to generate a whole plant from any cell or explant.\nTissue culture and micropropagation are based on this property.\nDifferentiation is only the specialisation of cells, not regeneration of an entire plant.\nSomatic hybridisation and emasculation are unrelated techniques.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Healthy plants can be recovered from virus-infected plants by culturing their meristems.\nReason (R): Even when a plant is infected with a virus, its apical and axillary meristems are free of the virus.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: in a virus-infected plant, the apical and axillary meristems remain free of the virus.\nA is true: meristems are removed and grown in vitro to obtain virus-free plants.\nThe culture yields healthy plants precisely because the starting meristem carries no virus. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about inbreeding in animal husbandry are correct?\n(i) It increases homozygosity and is needed to evolve pure lines.\n(ii) It exposes harmful recessive genes, which are then eliminated by selection.\n(iii) It is the mating of unrelated animals of the same breed for 4–6 generations.\n(iv) Continued close inbreeding usually reduces fertility and even productivity.\n(v) Inbreeding depression is reversed by mating selected animals with superior animals of a different species.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: inbreeding raises homozygosity, which is needed for pure lines.\n(ii) Correct: harmful recessive alleles become homozygous, show up and are removed by selection.\n(iii) Incorrect: inbreeding mates closely related animals; mating unrelated animals of the same breed is out-crossing.\n(iv) Correct: this loss of fertility and productivity is inbreeding depression.\n(v) Incorrect: the remedy uses unrelated superior animals of the same breed.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are freshwater fishes?\nCatla, Hilsa, Rohu, Sardine, Common carp, Pomfret, Mackerel","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Freshwater fishes named by NCERT: Catla, Rohu and common carp, which makes 3.\nMarine fishes named by NCERT: Hilsa, sardine, mackerel and pomfret.\nSo 3 of the listed fishes are freshwater.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (disease-resistant variety) with List-II (disease it resists).\nList-I\n(A) Himgiri (wheat)\n(B) Pusa Komal (cowpea)\n(C) Pusa Sadabahar (chilli)\n(D) Pusa Swarnim (Brassica)\nList-II\n(i) White rust\n(ii) Bacterial blight\n(iii) Chilly mosaic virus, tobacco mosaic virus and leaf curl\n(iv) Leaf and stripe rust, hill bunt","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Himgiri wheat resists leaf rust, stripe rust and hill bunt: A-iv.\nPusa Komal cowpea resists bacterial blight: B-ii.\nPusa Sadabahar chilli resists chilly mosaic virus, TMV and leaf curl: C-iii.\nPusa Swarnim (Karan rai) Brassica resists white rust: D-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about mutation breeding:\n(i) Mutations are induced by chemicals or by radiations such as gamma rays.\n(ii) Every induced mutant is released directly as a new variety, without selection.\n(iii) Resistance to yellow mosaic virus and powdery mildew in mung bean was obtained through induced mutations.\n(iv) Transferring a yellow mosaic virus resistance gene from a wild species into bhindi is an example of mutation breeding.\nWhich statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii)","(i) and (iii)","(iii) and (iv)","(i), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: chemicals and gamma radiation are used as mutagens.\n(ii) Incorrect: plants with the desirable character are first selected, then used directly or as a source in breeding.\n(iii) Correct: this is NCERT's mung bean example of induced resistance.\n(iv) Incorrect: Parbhani Kranti came from hybridisation with a wild relative, not from mutation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Spirulina\n(b) Methylophilus methylotrophus\n(c) Potato processing waste water\n(d) 3–10 kg\nList-II:\n(i) Starch-rich substrate for growing microbes\n(ii) Grain needed to produce 1 kg of meat\n(iii) Cyanobacterium grown as SCP\n(iv) Bacterium whose 250 g can yield about 25 tonnes of protein","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Spirulina is a cyanobacterium grown as SCP, so it matches (iii).\n(b) Methylophilus methylotrophus: 250 g can yield about 25 tonnes of protein, so it matches (iv).\n(c) Potato processing waste water contains starch and is used as a substrate, so it matches (i).\n(d) 3–10 kg is the grain needed per kg of meat, so it matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Continued close inbreeding usually reduces fertility and even productivity of animals.\nReason (R): Inbreeding increases homozygosity, which exposes harmful recessive genes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Continued inbreeding causes inbreeding depression, which lowers fertility and productivity.\nR is true. Inbreeding increases homozygosity, so recessive alleles come together.\nWhen harmful recessive genes become homozygous, their effects are expressed. This is the cause of inbreeding depression, so R correctly explains A.\nThe remedy is to mate the inbred animals with unrelated superior animals of the same breed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A dairy farmer in a hot, humid region imports a breed that gives very high milk yields in a cold temperate country. He houses, feeds and waters the animals well. However, they repeatedly fall sick and yield far less milk than expected. Which principle of dairy farm management did he most likely overlook?","image":"","options":["Fodder should be supplied in large quantity regardless of quality","Breeds should be selected for high yield potential under the climatic conditions of the area, along with disease resistance","Milking should be done manually rather than by machines","Record keeping is unnecessary when animals are well housed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT stresses selecting breeds with high yielding potential under the climatic conditions of the area, combined with disease resistance.\nThe farmer judged the breed by its yield in a different climate. In his region it is unsuited and falls sick, so its potential is never realised.\nThe other options misstate NCERT. Quality of fodder matters, machines are acceptable, and records are always useful.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A 250 kg cow produces about 200 g of protein per day. In the same period, 250 g of the bacterium Methylophilus methylotrophus can be expected to produce about:","image":"","options":["200 g of protein","25 kg of protein","2.5 tonnes of protein","25 tonnes of protein"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT compares the two directly: 250 g of Methylophilus methylotrophus can produce about 25 tonnes of protein in the time a 250 kg cow produces 200 g.\nThis huge yield comes from the microbe's very high rate of biomass production and growth.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Inbreeding increases homozygosity in a breed.\nReason (R): Continued close inbreeding over many generations usually increases fertility and productivity.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Inbreeding means mating more closely related individuals within the same breed for 4–6 generations. This increases homozygosity, which helps develop pure lines and exposes harmful recessive genes so they can be eliminated by selection.\nR is false. Continued inbreeding usually causes inbreeding depression, which lowers fertility and productivity.\nThis depression can be overcome by mating with unrelated superior animals of the same breed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A mule is produced by mating a female horse (mare) with a male donkey. This breeding method is called:","image":"","options":["Out-crossing","Cross-breeding","Interspecific hybridisation","Inbreeding"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Horse and donkey are two different but related species.\nMating a male and a female of two different species is called interspecific hybridisation.\nCross-breeding is the most tempting wrong option, but it involves different breeds of the same species.\nOut-crossing and inbreeding both take place within a single breed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Among the vegetable varieties released by IARI, New Delhi, which one appears in both the 'vitamin C enriched' list and the 'iron and calcium enriched' list?","image":"","options":["Spinach","Mustard","Tomato","Bathua"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The IARI lists are:\n- Vitamin A: carrot, spinach, pumpkin.\n- Vitamin C: bitter gourd, bathua, mustard, tomato.\n- Iron and calcium: spinach, bathua.\nBathua is the only vegetable in both the vitamin C and the iron and calcium lists.\nSpinach is tempting because it is also in two lists, but those are vitamin A and iron and calcium.\nMustard and tomato appear only under vitamin C.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about artificial insemination are correct?\n(i) The semen used comes from a male selected as a parent.\n(ii) Semen can be frozen and transported to the place where the female is housed.\n(iii) Artificial insemination helps overcome several problems of normal mating.\n(iv) Artificial insemination guarantees a successful pregnancy in every attempt.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: semen is taken from the male chosen as a parent.\n(ii) Correct: frozen semen can be stored and transported to where the female is kept.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT says AI helps overcome several problems of normal matings.\n(iv) Incorrect: the success rate often stays fairly low even with AI, which is why MOET is also used.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A 250 kg cow yields 200 g of protein per day. In the same time, 250 g of Methylophilus methylotrophus yields 25 tonnes of protein. How many times more protein does the microbe produce than the cow in this period? (1 tonne = $10^6$ g)","image":"","options":["$1.25 \\times 10^{3}$","$1.25 \\times 10^{4}$","$1.25 \\times 10^{5}$","$1.25 \\times 10^{6}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Microbe protein = 25 tonnes. Cow protein = 200 g.\nConvert units: 25 tonnes = $25 \\times 10^{6}$ g.\nFormula: Ratio = microbe protein ÷ cow protein.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{25 \\times 10^{6}}{200} = 1.25 \\times 10^{5}$.\nSo the microbe produces about $1.25 \\times 10^{5}$ times more protein than the cow.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Biofortification is considered the most practical means to improve public health.\nReason (R): Biofortification is the breeding of crops with higher levels of vitamins and minerals, or higher protein and healthier fats, so that the commonly eaten food itself supplies the missing nutrients.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT calls biofortification the most practical way to tackle hidden hunger and improve public health.\nR is true. It gives the correct definition of biofortification.\nR explains A: people already eat staple crops daily, so raising the nutrient content of those crops reaches people who cannot afford varied diets.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mutation breeding is useful for developing disease resistance in crops.\nReason (R): Conventional breeding for disease resistance is limited by the small number of resistance genes present and identified in crops and their wild relatives.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Inducing mutations creates new genetic variation, such as resistance to yellow mosaic virus and powdery mildew in mung bean.\nR is true. NCERT states that conventional breeding is constrained by the limited number of resistance genes available in crops and wild relatives.\nR explains A: mutation breeding is valuable because it generates resistance genes not found naturally.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In MOET, fertilised eggs are recovered from the donor cow and transferred to surrogate mothers. At what developmental stage are they recovered?","image":"","options":["Zygote (1-cell) stage","2-4 cell stage","8-32 cell stage","Blastocyst with a fully formed placenta"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, the fertilised eggs are recovered at the 8-32 cell stage.\nThey are recovered non-surgically and placed in surrogate mothers.\nThe zygote and 2-4 cell stages are too early.\nThe placenta forms only after implantation in the uterus, so the last option is impossible.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hilsa, sardines and pomfrets are marine fishes of commercial importance.\nStatement II: Common carp and mackerel are both freshwater fishes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT lists Hilsa, sardines, mackerel and pomfrets as popular marine fishes.\nStatement II is incorrect. Common carp is a freshwater fish, as are Catla and Rohu. Mackerel, however, is a marine fish.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement about the improvement of sugarcane in India is correct?","image":"","options":["Saccharum barberi, grown in south India, had a high sugar content and thick stems","Saccharum officinarum had thicker stems and higher sugar content but did not grow well in north India","The hybrid of the two species lost the ability to grow in north India","Saccharum officinarum was native to north India and had a poor yield"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"S. barberi was grown in north India but had a poor sugar content and yield, so options (1) and (4) are wrong.\nS. officinarum was the tropical canemof south India. It had thicker stems and higher sugar but did not grow well in north India, so (2) is correct.\nThe hybrid combined high yield, thick stems and high sugar with the ability to grow in north India, so (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A plant breeder collects and preserves seeds and plants of all cultivated varieties of rice, along with their wild species and relatives. The aim is to have all the diverse alleles of all genes in the crop available for later use. This first step of a breeding programme is called:","image":"","options":["Evaluation and selection of parents","Germplasm collection","Selection of superior recombinants","Commercialisation of cultivars"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Breeding starts with collection of variability, because genetic variability is the root of any breeding programme.\nThe whole collection of plants or seeds that carries all the diverse alleles of all genes of a crop is called germplasm collection.\nEvaluation and selection of parents is the next step. It screens this collection for plants with desirable character combinations, so option (1) does not fit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which biochemical composition of maize gives resistance to maize stem borers?","image":"","options":["High aspartic acid, high nitrogen and high sugar content","Low aspartic acid, low nitrogen and high sugar content","High aspartic acid, low nitrogen and low sugar content","Low aspartic acid, high nitrogen and low sugar content"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT gives a biochemical basis of resistance in maize: high aspartic acid together with low nitrogen and low sugar content makes maize resistant to stem borers.\nAny option with high nitrogen or high sugar is wrong. These would make the plant more nutritious for the borer.\nAny option with low aspartic acid is also wrong.\nOnly option (3) has all three conditions right.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Agriculture accounts for approximately 33% of India's Gross Domestic Product.\nStatement II: Agriculture employs approximately 33% of India's population.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Agriculture contributes about 33% of India's GDP.\nStatement II is incorrect. Agriculture employs nearly 62% of the population, not 33%.\nThe statements swap the two figures, a common confusion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Pusa Gaurav is an insect-resistant variety of Brassica (rapeseed mustard). It was developed for resistance against:","image":"","options":["Shoot and fruit borer","Cereal leaf beetle","Aphids","Stem sawfly"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"According to the NCERT table of insect-resistant varieties, Pusa Gaurav (Brassica) is resistant to aphids.\nShoot and fruit borer resistance belongs to the okra varieties Pusa Sawani and Pusa A-4.\nCereal leaf beetle and stem sawfly are pests of wheat, not mustard.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Hisardale is a new breed of sheep developed in Punjab. It was produced by crossing:","image":"","options":["Bikaneri ewes with Marino rams, an example of cross-breeding","Marino ewes with Bikaneri rams, an example of inbreeding","Bikaneri ewes with Marino rams, an example of interspecific hybridisation","Bikaneri ewes with Bikaneri rams having no common ancestor for 4–6 generations, an example of out-crossing"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT gives Hisardale as the example of cross-breeding.\nIn cross-breeding, superior males of one breed are mated with superior females of another breed.\nHisardale came from Bikaneri ewes (females) and Marino rams (males).\nBikaneri and Marino are two breeds of the same species, sheep. So it is not interspecific hybridisation.\nOut-crossing uses animals of the same breed, and inbreeding uses closely related animals, so neither fits.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"The following steps are involved in producing a somatic hybrid such as pomato:\n(P) Regeneration of a whole plant from the hybrid cell on a nutrient medium\n(Q) Treatment of cells with cellulase and pectinase\n(R) Selection of cells from tomato and potato plants\n(S) Fusion of the naked protoplasts to form a hybrid protoplast\nChoose the correct sequence.","image":"","options":["R → S → Q → P","Q → R → S → P","R → Q → P → S","R → Q → S → P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1: Cells are first taken from the two parent plants (R).\nStep 2: Their walls are digested with cellulase and pectinase to free the protoplasts (Q).\nStep 3: The naked protoplasts are fused to form a hybrid protoplast (S). Fusion is not possible while the walls are still present, which rules out option 1.\nStep 4: The hybrid cell is cultured in a nutrient medium and regenerates into a plant (P), using its totipotency.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about plant tissue culture are correct?\n(i) The nutrient medium contains growth regulators such as auxins and cytokinins.\n(ii) Plants produced by micropropagation show wide genetic variation among themselves.\n(iii) Tomato, banana and apple have been produced on a commercial scale by micropropagation.\n(iv) Vitamins and amino acids are part of the nutrient medium.\n(v) Explants must be grown under sterile conditions.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: auxins and cytokinins are added to the medium as growth regulators.\n(ii) Incorrect: micropropagated plants are somaclones and are genetically identical, not variable.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT cites tomato, banana and apple as commercially micropropagated crops.\n(iv) Correct: the medium also supplies vitamins and amino acids.\n(v) Correct: explants are cultured under sterile conditions.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are marine fishes?\nHilsa, Sardines, Rohu, Mackerel, Catla, Pomfret, Common carp","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Marine: Hilsa, Sardines, Mackerel and Pomfret (NCERT's examples of common marine fishes).\nFreshwater: Catla, Rohu and Common carp.\nThat makes 4 marine fishes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In somatic hybridisation, protoplasts taken from two different plants, each having a desirable character, can be fused and grown into a new hybrid plant.\nStatement II: Pomato, produced by fusing tomato and potato protoplasts, combined all the desired characters and is cultivated commercially on a large scale.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Fusing protoplasts from two different plants gives hybrid protoplasts, which grow into somatic hybrids.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT notes that pomato did not have all the desired combination of characters needed for commercial use.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"When a population shifts from grain-based to meat-based diets, the total demand for cereals rises rather than falls. Which of the following best explains this?","image":"","options":["Livestock store only a small fraction of the energy in the grain they eat as meat; most is lost in their own metabolism, so several kg of grain are needed per kg of meat","Meat contains less protein than cereals, so more grain must be eaten alongside it","Microbes used for single cell protein must be grown on cereal grains","Grain fed to animals is mostly converted into milk instead of meat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that producing 1 kg of meat by animal farming needs 3–10 kg of grain.\nThis follows from energy flow: livestock are a higher trophic level, and much of the energy in their feed is lost in respiration and maintenance.\nSo meat production consumes far more grain than eating the grain directly would.\nOption 2 is wrong because meat is not poorer in protein. Option 3 is wrong because SCP microbes use wastes such as molasses, straw and sewage.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Crossing Saccharum barberi with Saccharum officinarum gave sugarcane with high yield, thick stems and high sugar content that could be grown in north India.\nReason (R): Saccharum officinarum, the tropical cane, grows well in the sugarcane areas of north India.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The cross combined the desirable traits of both canes with the ability to grow in north India.\nR is false. S. officinarum has thick stems and high sugar content, but it did not grow well in north India.\nS. barberi grew well in north India but had poor sugar content and yield. That is why the two species were crossed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about biofortification are correct?\n(i) Wheat variety Atlas 66, having high protein content, has been used as a donor for improving cultivated wheat.\n(ii) Maize hybrids have been developed with twice the amount of lysine and tryptophan compared to existing hybrids.\n(iii) An iron-fortified rice variety contains about five times the iron of commonly consumed varieties.\n(iv) IARI has released vitamin C-enriched carrots.\n(v) IARI has released spinach and bathua enriched in iron and calcium.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Atlas 66 is a high-protein wheat used as a donor parent.\n(ii) Correct: these maize hybrids have twice the lysine and tryptophan.\n(iii) Correct: the iron-fortified rice has about five times more iron.\n(iv) Incorrect: the carrots were enriched in vitamin A. The vitamin C-enriched crops are bitter gourd, bathua, mustard and tomato.\n(v) Correct: spinach and bathua were enriched in iron and calcium.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about plant tissue culture and somatic hybridisation are correct?\n(i) Any part of a plant taken out and grown in a test tube under sterile conditions in a nutrient medium is called an explant.\n(ii) Plants produced by micropropagation are genetically identical to the original plant and are called somaclones.\n(iii) Protoplasts are isolated by digesting plant cell walls with cellulase and pectinase.\n(iv) The somatic hybrid pomato has all the desired characteristics needed for its commercial use.\n(v) Virus-free plants can be raised from apical and axillary meristems of an infected plant.\n(vi) The culture medium must supply a carbon source such as sucrose, inorganic salts, vitamins, amino acids and growth regulators such as auxins and cytokinins.","image":"","options":["6","5","4","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the NCERT definition of an explant.\n(ii) Correct: micropropagated plants are somaclones, genetically identical to the source plant.\n(iii) Correct: cellulase and pectinase remove the cell wall to release protoplasts.\n(iv) Incorrect: pomato did not have all the desired characters for commercial use.\n(v) Correct: meristems remain virus-free even in an infected plant.\n(vi) Correct: these are the medium components listed in NCERT.\nSo five statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (component of poultry farm management) with List-II (practice):\nList-I\n(A) Selection of breed\n(B) Safe farm conditions\n(C) Feed and water\n(D) Hygiene and health care\nList-II\n(I) Well-ventilated, protected housing that is not overcrowded\n(II) Regular cleaning of sheds, vaccination and veterinary check-ups\n(III) Choosing disease-free birds suited to local conditions\n(IV) Balanced, good-quality feed with a clean drinking supply","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Breed selection means choosing disease-free birds suited to local conditions, so A-III.\n(B) Safe farm conditions mean ventilated, protected, uncrowded housing, so B-I.\n(C) Feed and water mean balanced quality feed and clean drinking water, so C-IV.\n(D) Hygiene and health care cover cleaning, vaccination and vet visits, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (breeding method) with List-II (description/example):\nList-I\n(a) Out-crossing\n(b) Cross-breeding\n(c) Interspecific hybridisation\n(d) Inbreeding\nList-II\n(i) Mare × male donkey\n(ii) Bikaneri ewe × Marino ram\n(iii) Mating of closely related animals of the same breed for 4–6 generations\n(iv) Mating within the same breed of animals with no common ancestors on either side for 4–6 generations","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Out-crossing means the same breed but no common ancestors for 4–6 generations, so (a)-(iv).\n(b) Cross-breeding uses two different breeds, as in Bikaneri ewe × Marino ram (Hisardale), so (b)-(ii).\n(c) Interspecific hybridisation uses two species, as in mare × donkey giving a mule, so (c)-(i).\n(d) Inbreeding means mating closely related animals of the same breed, so (d)-(iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"The capacity of a plant cell or explant to regenerate into a complete plant is called:","image":"","options":["Pluripotency","Totipotency","Differentiation","Apomixis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines totipotency as the capacity to generate a whole plant from any cell or explant.\nPlant tissue culture is based on this property.\nPluripotency is a term mostly used for animal stem cells. It does not mean regeneration of a whole organism.\nApomixis is seed formation without fertilisation. Differentiation is cell specialisation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Jaya and Ratna are best described as:","image":"","options":["Semi-dwarf wheat varieties introduced from Mexico in 1963","Better-yielding semi-dwarf rice varieties developed in India","Hybrid bajra varieties resistant to water stress","Sugarcane varieties from a cross of Saccharum barberi and Saccharum officinarum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Semi-dwarf rice derived from IR-8 and Taichung Native-1 was introduced in India in 1966.\nLater, better-yielding semi-dwarf rice varieties named Jaya and Ratna were developed within India.\nOption (1) wrongly describes them as Mexican wheat. The wheat varieties introduced in 1963 were Sonalika and Kalyan Sona.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Pusa Sadabahar, a chilli variety, is resistant to chilly mosaic virus, tobacco mosaic virus and leaf curl.\nStatement II: Pusa Shubhra, a Brassica variety, is resistant to white rust.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Pusa Sadabahar (chilli) resists chilly mosaic virus, TMV and leaf curl.\nStatement II is incorrect on two counts. Pusa Shubhra is a cauliflower variety, and it resists black rot and curl blight black rot.\nThe Brassica variety resistant to white rust is Pusa Swarnim (Karan rai).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Keeping beehives in crop fields during the flowering period improves crop yield as well as honey yield.\nReason (R): Honeybees are pollinators of many crop species such as sunflower, $Brassica$, apple and pear.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that hives placed in fields during flowering raise pollination efficiency, which benefits the crop yield. The bees also get nectar, which benefits the honey yield.\nR is true. Bees pollinate sunflower, $Brassica$, apple and pear.\nThe pollinating role of bees is exactly why crop yield rises, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"The sheep breed Hisardale, developed in Punjab, is the result of crossing which of the following?","image":"","options":["Marino ewes with Bikaneri rams","Bikaneri ewes with Marino rams","Bikaneri ewes with Bikaneri rams that had no common ancestors","Marino ewes with a related goat species"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hisardale is the NCERT example of cross-breeding. Cross-breeding means mating superior males of one breed with superior females of another breed.\nThe females used were Bikaneri ewes and the males were Marino rams.\nOption (1) reverses the sexes of the two parent breeds, so it is wrong.\nOption (3) describes out-crossing within a single breed, not the cross that produced Hisardale.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (breeding method) with List-II (description):\nList-I\nA. Inbreeding\nB. Out-crossing\nC. Cross-breeding\nD. Interspecific hybridisation\nList-II\n(i) Superior males of one breed mated with superior females of another breed\n(ii) Mating of males and females of two different related species\n(iii) Mating of more closely related individuals within the same breed for 4–6 generations\n(iv) Mating within the same breed with no common ancestors on either side up to 4–6 generations","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–(iii): Inbreeding mates closely related animals of the same breed for 4–6 generations.\nB–(iv): Out-crossing mates animals of the same breed that have no common ancestors.\nC–(i): Cross-breeding mates superior males of one breed with superior females of another breed, as in Hisardale.\nD–(ii): Interspecific hybridisation mates two related species, as in the mule.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair of okra (bhindi) varieties was developed for resistance to shoot and fruit borer?","image":"","options":["Pusa Sem 2 and Pusa Sem 3","Pusa Sawani and Pusa A-4","Pusa Gaurav and Pusa Swarnim","Pusa Komal and Pusa Sadabahar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The NCERT table lists Pusa Sawani and Pusa A-4 as okra varieties resistant to shoot and fruit borer.\nPusa Sem 2 and Pusa Sem 3 are flat bean varieties.\nPusa Gaurav and Pusa Swarnim are Brassica varieties.\nPusa Komal (cowpea) and Pusa Sadabahar (chilli) are disease-resistant varieties, not insect-resistant ones.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In the Multiple Ovulation Embryo Transfer (MOET) technology used for herd improvement, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A cow is given hormones with FSH-like activity to induce follicular maturation and super-ovulation","About 6–8 eggs are produced per cycle instead of the usual single egg","Fertilised eggs at the 8–32 cell stage are recovered surgically and returned to the same genetic mother","The technique has been demonstrated for cattle, sheep, rabbits, buffaloes and mares"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: hormones with FSH-like activity induce super-ovulation.\n(2) Correct: 6–8 eggs are released per cycle instead of one.\n(3) Incorrect: embryos at the 8–32 cell stage are recovered non-surgically and transferred to surrogate mothers. The genetic mother is then available for another round of super-ovulation.\n(4) Correct: NCERT lists cattle, sheep, rabbits, buffaloes and mares.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Jersey and Sahiwal are two kinds of cattle. Each is a group of animals related by descent and similar in general appearance, size and configuration. Jersey and Sahiwal are therefore best described as:","image":"","options":["Two different species of the same genus","Two different breeds of the same species","Two pure lines of the same breed","Two interspecific hybrids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines a breed as a group of animals related by descent and similar in most characters, such as general appearance, features, size and configuration.\nJersey and Sahiwal are both cattle, so they belong to the same species.\nThey are therefore different breeds of that one species.\nThey are not separate species, because they belong to the same species and can be cross-bred to give hybrid cattle.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about biofortification are correct?\n(i) Wheat variety Atlas 66, which has a high protein content, has been used as a donor for improving cultivated wheat.\n(ii) Maize hybrids have been developed that contain twice the amount of lysine and tryptophan compared to existing hybrids.\n(iii) Iron-fortified rice containing over five times the iron of commonly consumed varieties has been developed.\n(iv) IARI has released bitter gourd varieties enriched mainly in vitamin A.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Atlas 66, a high-protein wheat, was used as a donor to improve cultivated wheat.\n(ii) Correct: maize hybrids with twice the lysine and tryptophan have been developed.\n(iii) Correct: iron-fortified rice has over five times the iron of common varieties.\n(iv) Incorrect: IARI's bitter gourd varieties are rich in vitamin C. The vitamin A-rich varieties are carrots, spinach and pumpkin.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following steps of a plant breeding programme in the correct order:\nA. Evaluation and selection of parents\nB. Testing, release and commercialisation of new cultivars\nC. Collection of variability\nD. Selection and testing of superior recombinants\nE. Cross hybridisation among the selected parents","image":"","options":["C → A → E → D → B","A → C → E → D → B","C → E → A → D → B","C → A → D → E → B"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1 is collection of variability, i.e., making a germplasm collection (C).\nStep 2 is evaluating the germplasm and selecting parents with the desired traits (A).\nStep 3 is cross hybridisation among the selected parents (E).\nStep 4 is selecting superior recombinants and testing them (D).\nStep 5 is testing, release and commercialisation of the new cultivar (B).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In a plant tissue culture medium, which compound does NCERT name as the carbon source for the growing explant?","image":"","options":["Cellulose","Urea","Sucrose","Chitin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Explants growing in vitro cannot meet their carbon needs by photosynthesis alone, so carbon must be supplied in the medium.\nNCERT names sucrose as the carbon source. The medium also contains inorganic salts, vitamins, amino acids, auxins and cytokinins.\nCellulose and chitin are structural polymers that plant cells cannot readily use.\nUrea is a nitrogenous compound, not a carbon source.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plant diseases are caused by fungi?\nBrown rust of wheat, Red rot of sugarcane, Late blight of potato, Black rot of crucifers, Tobacco mosaic, Turnip mosaic","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fungal diseases: brown rust of wheat, red rot of sugarcane and late blight of potato. That makes 3.\nBlack rot of crucifers is caused by a bacterium.\nTobacco mosaic and turnip mosaic are caused by viruses.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A dairy farm kept a pure line of a superior cattle breed by close inbreeding for many generations. The herd now shows falling fertility and lower milk yield. The owner wants to restore fertility and yield without changing the breed of the herd. Which approach is most appropriate?","image":"","options":["Continue close inbreeding with stricter selection so that harmful recessive genes are removed","Cross the cows with superior bulls of a different breed to produce hybrids","Mate selected animals of the herd with unrelated superior animals of the same breed","Perform interspecific hybridisation with a related species to introduce new genes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The falling fertility and yield after long close inbreeding indicate inbreeding depression.\nNCERT's remedy is to mate selected animals with unrelated superior animals of the same breed. This is out-crossing, and it keeps the breed unchanged.\nOption 1 would make the depression worse.\nOption 2 is cross-breeding, which mixes two breeds and changes the herd's breed.\nOption 4 crosses species and produces a hybrid rather than the original breed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about micropropagation are correct?\n(i) Thousands of plants can be produced in a very short time.\n(ii) The plants produced are genetically identical to the original plant.\n(iii) The explant is grown under sterile conditions.\n(iv) The plants produced show wide genetic variation from the parent.\n(v) Tomato, banana and apple have been produced on a commercial scale by this method.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: rapid mass multiplication is the main advantage of micropropagation.\n(ii) Correct: the plants are somaclones of the parent.\n(iii) Correct: aseptic conditions prevent microbial contamination of the medium.\n(iv) Incorrect: the plants are uniform copies of the parent, not variants.\n(v) Correct: NCERT cites tomato, banana and apple.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, roughly how much grain is needed to produce 1 kg of meat by animal farming?","image":"","options":["1–2 kg","3–10 kg","15–20 kg","25–30 kg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that 3–10 kg of grain is needed to produce 1 kg of meat by animal farming.\nThis is why a shift from grain-based to meat-based diets raises the demand for cereals.\nThe rising demand is one reason alternative protein sources such as SCP are being explored.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following plant diseases is caused by a bacterium?","image":"","options":["Brown rust of wheat","Black rot of crucifers","Late blight of potato","Turnip mosaic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT groups plant pathogens as fungi, bacteria and viruses.\nBlack rot of crucifers is its example of a bacterial disease.\nBrown rust of wheat and late blight of potato are given as fungal diseases.\nTurnip mosaic is a viral disease, like tobacco mosaic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about biofortification are correct?\n(i) Maize hybrids have been developed with twice the amount of lysine and tryptophan compared with existing hybrids.\n(ii) The wheat variety Atlas 66 has a high protein content and has served as a donor for improving cultivated wheat.\n(iii) An iron-fortified rice variety contains over five times as much iron as commonly consumed varieties.\n(iv) IARI has released bitter gourd and tomato varieties enriched mainly in vitamin A.\n(v) Carrots and pumpkin have been biofortified mainly for protein.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: these maize hybrids have twice the lysine and tryptophan.\n(ii) Correct: Atlas 66 is a high-protein wheat used as a donor.\n(iii) Correct: the fortified rice has over five times the iron.\n(iv) Incorrect: bitter gourd and tomato varieties were enriched in vitamin C.\n(v) Incorrect: carrots and pumpkin were enriched in vitamin A. The protein-enriched crops are beans such as broad, lablab and French beans.\nCorrect statements = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about breeding for improved food quality are correct?\n(i) The number of people suffering from hidden hunger is greater than the number who lack adequate food.\n(ii) Iodine and zinc are among the micronutrients whose deficiency in diets increases the risk of disease and reduces mental abilities.\n(iii) The biofortified rice variety contains about twice as much iron as common varieties.\n(iv) Atlas 66 is a high-protein rice variety used as a donor parent.\n(v) Developing crops with healthier fats is a part of biofortification.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: about three billion people suffer hidden hunger, compared with over 840 million who lack adequate food.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT names iron, vitamin A, iodine and zinc.\n(iii) Incorrect: the rice has over five times the iron, not twice.\n(iv) Incorrect: Atlas 66 is a wheat variety, not rice.\n(v) Correct: biofortification includes higher protein and healthier fats.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"The mule, an economically valuable animal, is produced by interspecific hybridisation between:","image":"","options":["Male horse and female donkey","Female horse and male donkey","Bikaneri ewe and Marino ram","Two different breeds of horse"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A mule is the progeny of a female horse (mare) and a male donkey.\nThe two parents belong to two different but related species, so this is interspecific hybridisation.\nThe reverse cross, a male horse with a female donkey, is not the mule described in NCERT.\nA Bikaneri ewe crossed with a Marino ram gives Hisardale sheep, which is cross-breeding.\nCrossing two breeds of horse is also cross-breeding, because both parents belong to the same species.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Dairying is best defined as:","image":"","options":["Rearing of cattle only for draught purposes","Management of animals for milk and its products for human consumption","Breeding of poultry for eggs and meat","Processing of milk in factories without involving animals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines dairying as the management of animals for milk and its products for human consumption.\nRearing animals for draught is not dairying.\nPoultry farming is a separate branch of animal husbandry.\nDairying involves managing the animals themselves, not just processing milk.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"More than 70 per cent of the world's livestock population is found in which country or countries?","image":"","options":["India alone","China alone","India and China together","USA and Brazil together"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that India and China together hold more than 70% of the world's livestock population.\nNeither country reaches this share alone, so 'India alone' and 'China alone' are both wrong.\nUSA and Brazil are not mentioned as holding this share.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about plant breeding are correct?\n(i) Only about one in a few hundred to a thousand crosses shows the desired combination of characters.\n(ii) Progeny superior to both parents are self-pollinated for several generations until they become uniform (homozygous).\n(iii) A new line is compared with the best available local cultivar, which serves as a check or reference.\n(iv) Emasculation is done after the anthers dehisce, so that the flower's own pollen is used up first.\n(v) In research fields, new lines are evaluated under ideal fertiliser application and irrigation.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Hybridisation is tedious, and desirable recombinants are rare.\n(ii) Correct. Repeated selfing makes the selected lines uniform, so the characters do not segregate.\n(iii) Correct. The local check cultivar is the benchmark for judging the new line.\n(iv) Incorrect. Anthers are removed before they dehisce, so that self-pollination is prevented. The flower is then bagged.\n(v) Correct. Yield, quality and disease resistance are first recorded under ideal management.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the type of pathogen behind the disease(s) each variety resists. Which one of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Pusa Komal cowpea is resistant to a disease caused by a bacterium.","All the diseases to which Pusa Sadabahar chilli is resistant are caused by viruses.","Himgiri wheat is resistant to diseases caused by fungi.","Pusa Shubhra cauliflower is resistant to black rot, which is caused by a virus."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Pusa Komal resists bacterial blight, a bacterial disease.\n(2) Correct: Pusa Sadabahar resists chilly mosaic virus, tobacco mosaic virus and leaf curl. All three are viral.\n(3) Correct: Himgiri resists leaf rust, stripe rust and hill bunt. All three are fungal.\n(4) Incorrect: Pusa Shubhra (like Pusa Snowball K-1) does resist black rot, but black rot of crucifers is bacterial, not viral.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Maize hybrids (2000)\n(b) Atlas 66\n(c) Biofortified rice variety\n(d) IARI carrot variety\nList-II\n(i) Over five times more iron than common varieties\n(ii) Enriched in vitamin A\n(iii) Twice the lysine and tryptophan\n(iv) High protein; used as a donor for improving cultivated wheat","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) The maize hybrids of 2000 had twice the lysine and tryptophan, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Atlas 66 is a high-protein wheat used as a donor for improving cultivated wheat, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) The iron-fortified rice has over five times the iron of common varieties, so (c)-(i).\n(d) IARI released vitamin A enriched carrots, so (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Smooth-leaved and nectar-less varieties of cotton suffer less damage from bollworms.\nReason (R): Nectar-less cotton varieties produce toxic proteins in their bolls that kill bollworm larvae.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that smooth-leaved and nectar-less cotton varieties do not attract bollworms.\nR is false. These varieties resist bollworms because they lack the features that attract the insect. They do not make toxins.\nToxin-based resistance to bollworms is seen in Bt cotton, which is produced by genetic engineering. It is not explained by nectar-less leaves.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements on plant tissue culture and somatic hybridisation:\n(i) Pomato, a somatic hybrid of tomato and potato, combines all the desirable characteristics needed for commercial use.\n(ii) Naked protoplasts are obtained by digesting plant cell walls with enzymes.\n(iii) Plants produced by micropropagation are genetically identical to the original plant and are called somaclones.\n(iv) An explant must always be taken from the shoot apical meristem.\n(v) The capacity to generate a whole plant from any cell or explant is called totipotency.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(ii), (iv) and (v) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only","(ii), (iii) and (v) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: pomato does not have all the desired combination of characteristics needed for commercial use.\n(ii) Correct: enzymes digest the cell wall to release protoplasts, which can then be fused.\n(iii) Correct: micropropagated plants are genetically identical somaclones.\n(iv) Incorrect: any part of a plant can serve as an explant. Meristems are used specifically to obtain virus-free plants.\n(v) Correct: this is the definition of totipotency.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following groups contains only protein-enriched beans released by the Indian Agricultural Research Institute (IARI), New Delhi?","image":"","options":["Broad bean, lablab, carrot and garden pea","Broad bean, lablab, French bean and garden pea","Lablab, bitter gourd, French bean and tomato","Garden pea, pumpkin, broad bean and mustard"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"IARI released several vegetable varieties rich in vitamins, minerals or protein.\nThe protein-enriched beans are broad bean, lablab, French bean and garden pea.\nCarrot and pumpkin were released for vitamin A, not protein.\nBitter gourd, tomato and mustard were released for vitamin C.\nEach of the other options therefore contains at least one vegetable from a different category.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During bird flu outbreaks, lakhs of chickens in and around affected areas were culled (killed) by authorities.\nReason (R): Culling removes infected and exposed birds, which checks the spread of the H5N1 virus to other flocks and to humans.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: millions of birds were culled in states such as West Bengal, Assam, Orissa and Maharashtra during outbreaks.\nR is true: killing infected and potentially exposed birds removes the source of the virus.\nThis stops transmission to healthy flocks and lowers the risk to people.\nR therefore explains why culling was done.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Thousands of plants raised by micropropagation from a single parent plant are genetically identical to it. Such plants are called:","image":"","options":["Somatic hybrids","Somaclones","Transgenic plants","Pure lines"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Micropropagation uses only mitotic divisions of the explant's cells, so the plants produced are genetically identical to the original plant.\nNCERT calls these plants somaclones.\nSomatic hybrids come from the fusion of two different protoplasts, so they are not identical to either parent.\nTransgenic plants carry foreign genes.\nPure lines are produced by repeated self-pollination, not by tissue culture.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II (approximate values as given in NCERT).\nList-I\n(A) Share of India's GDP contributed by agriculture\n(B) Share of India's population employed in agriculture\n(C) Share of world livestock population found in India and China together\n(D) Share of world farm produce contributed by India and China together\nList-II\n(i) 25 per cent\n(ii) 33 per cent\n(iii) more than 70 per cent\n(iv) about 62 per cent","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Agriculture contributes about 33% of India's GDP, so A matches (ii).\n(B) It employs nearly 62% of the population, so B matches (iv).\n(C) India and China together hold more than 70% of the world's livestock, so C matches (iii).\n(D) Their share of world farm produce is only about 25%, so D matches (i).\nThe gap between (C) and (D) shows that productivity per animal is low. This is why better breeds and better management are needed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Pomato was developed by fusing protoplasts of tomato and potato.\nStatement II: Pomato combined all the desired characteristics needed for its commercial utilisation.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Isolated tomato and potato protoplasts were fused, and the hybrid protoplast was grown into the somatic hybrid called pomato.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT notes that pomato did not have all the desired combination of characteristics for commercial use.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In artificial insemination used for controlled breeding, the semen collected from the chosen male is:","image":"","options":["Always used within an hour, because it cannot be stored","Injected into the reproductive tract of the selected female, either immediately or after being frozen","Mixed with eggs in a culture dish, and the zygote is placed in the female","Injected into the bloodstream of the female to reach the ovary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In AI, the breeder collects semen from the male chosen as a parent.\nThe breeder injects it into the reproductive tract of the selected female.\nThe semen can be used at once, or frozen and used later, so option 1 is wrong.\nOption 3 describes in vitro fertilisation, not AI.\nSemen is never injected into the blood.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"The semi-dwarf rice variety IR-8, whose derivatives were introduced in India in 1966, was developed at:","image":"","options":["International Centre for Wheat and Maize Improvement, Mexico","Taiwan","International Rice Research Institute (IRRI), Philippines","Indian Agricultural Research Institute, New Delhi"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that Indian semi-dwarf rice varieties were derived from two sources.\nIR-8 was developed at the International Rice Research Institute (IRRI), Philippines.\nTaichung Native-1 came from Taiwan, which makes option (2) a tempting but wrong choice.\nThe Mexican centre is linked to Borlaug's semi-dwarf wheat, not to IR-8.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In MOET, embryos are recovered from the donor cow non-surgically.\nStatement II: MOET is limited to cattle and has not been demonstrated in other animals.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that the fertilised eggs at the 8-32 cell stage are recovered non-surgically.\nStatement II is incorrect: MOET has been demonstrated in cattle, sheep, rabbits, buffaloes, mares and other animals.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A breeder crosses two selected parents. Among several hundred F1 plants, she finds one hybrid that combines the desirable traits of both parents and outperforms each of them. What should be her next step before the line can be tested as a new cultivar?","image":"","options":["Release its seeds to farmers immediately, since it is already superior","Self-pollinate it for several generations until it becomes homozygous and uniform","Back-cross it repeatedly with the less productive parent","Start a fresh germplasm collection from its wild relatives"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A superior hybrid is heterozygous, so its progeny would segregate and lose the desired combination of traits.\nNCERT states that such recombinants are selfed for several generations until they reach uniformity (homozygosity).\nOnly after this is the line evaluated and tested in farmers' fields.\nImmediate release (option 1) would give non-uniform crops, and the cultivar would still have to clear field trials first.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Choose the correct pairing of the uses of two honeybee products as described in NCERT.","image":"","options":["Honey – preparation of polishes; Beeswax – indigenous systems of medicine","Honey – food of high nutritive value and indigenous medicine; Beeswax – cosmetics and polishes","Honey – cosmetics only; Beeswax – food of high nutritive value","Honey – industrial lubricant; Beeswax – indigenous medicine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Honey is a food of high nutritive value and is also used in indigenous systems of medicine.\nBeeswax has many industrial uses, such as preparing cosmetics and polishes of various kinds.\nOption (1) is tempting because it names both uses correctly, but it assigns them to the wrong products.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A tomato leaf explant is micropropagated to give plant X. Separately, a tomato protoplast is fused with a potato protoplast and the product is grown into plant Y. Which comparison is correct?","image":"","options":["Both X and Y are genetically identical to the tomato parent because both arise by tissue culture","X is genetically identical to the tomato parent, whereas Y carries genetic material of both tomato and potato","X carries genes of both parents, whereas Y is a somaclone of tomato","Both X and Y arise by sexual fusion of gametes in vitro"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Plant X comes from cells of a single tomato plant that divided only by mitosis. It is therefore a somaclone, genetically identical to the tomato parent.\nPlant Y comes from the fusion of two somatic protoplasts. It contains genetic material of both tomato and potato, so it is a somatic hybrid.\nOption 1 wrongly assumes that every tissue-culture plant is a clone.\nOption 4 fails because no gametes are involved in either process.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, approximately what percentage of India's population is employed in agriculture?","image":"","options":["About 33%","About 50%","About 62%","About 80%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that agriculture employs nearly 62% of India's population.\nThe figure 33% is the tempting wrong option. It is agriculture's share of India's GDP, not its share of employment.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (variety) with List-II (resistance):\nList-I\n(a) Himgiri\n(b) Pusa Swarnim\n(c) Pusa Snowball K-1\n(d) Pusa Sadabahar\nList-II\n(i) White rust\n(ii) Chilly mosaic virus, TMV and leaf curl\n(iii) Leaf and stripe rust, hill bunt\n(iv) Black rot and curl blight black rot","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Himgiri is a wheat variety resistant to leaf and stripe rust and hill bunt, so (iii).\n(b) Pusa Swarnim (Karan rai, Brassica) resists white rust, so (i).\n(c) Pusa Snowball K-1 is a cauliflower variety resistant to black rot and curl blight black rot, so (iv).\n(d) Pusa Sadabahar is a chilli variety resistant to chilly mosaic virus, TMV and leaf curl, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT among the materials NCERT lists for growing Spirulina on a large scale?","image":"","options":["Waste water from potato processing plants","Molasses","Fly ash from thermal power plants","Animal manure and sewage"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists these materials for growing Spirulina: waste water from potato processing plants (which contains starch), straw, molasses, animal manure and even sewage.\nAll of these are organic wastes or by-products.\nFly ash is an inorganic combustion residue and is not mentioned as a growth medium.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In mutation breeding, which of the following is used to change the base sequence of genes in crop plants?","image":"","options":["Gamma radiation","Cellulase and pectinase","Colchicine-free emasculation","Bagging of flowers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mutation breeding induces mutations with chemicals or radiations such as gamma radiation.\nPlants showing the desired trait are then selected and used as a source in breeding.\nCellulase and pectinase digest cell walls to isolate protoplasts. They do not cause mutations.\nEmasculation and bagging are steps of artificial hybridisation, not mutagenesis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (biofortified crop) with List-II (nutrient for which it was enriched):\nList-I\nA. Carrot\nB. Bitter gourd\nC. Lablab bean\nD. Rice\nList-II\nI. Protein\nII. Iron (over five times that of common varieties)\nIII. Vitamin A\nIV. Vitamin C\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Carrot: IARI released carrots enriched in vitamin A, so A-III.\nB. Bitter gourd: listed with bathua, mustard and tomato as vitamin C enriched, so B-IV.\nC. Lablab bean: included in the protein-enriched beans (broad, lablab, French beans and garden peas), so C-I.\nD. Rice: an iron-fortified variety with over five times as much iron as commonly consumed varieties was developed, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about breeding for insect pest resistance is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Sources of resistance genes can be cultivated varieties, germplasm collections or wild relatives of the crop.","The main steps of breeding for insect resistance are the same as those for breeding any other agronomic trait.","Pusa Gaurav is a flat bean variety that resists jassids, aphids and fruit borer.","Hairy leaves in wheat give resistance to the cereal leaf beetle."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT lists cultivated varieties, germplasm and wild relatives as sources of resistance genes.\n(2) Correct: insect resistance is bred using the same steps as any other trait (collection, evaluation, hybridisation, selection, testing and release).\n(3) Incorrect: Pusa Gaurav is a Brassica variety resistant to aphids. The flat bean varieties resistant to jassids, aphids and fruit borer are Pusa Sem 2 and Pusa Sem 3.\n(4) Correct: hairy leaves in wheat protect against the cereal leaf beetle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A banana plant is infected with a virus. To recover healthy, virus-free plants from it by tissue culture, the most suitable explant is:","image":"","options":["Mature leaf mesophyll","Apical or axillary meristem","Phloem tissue of the stem","Root cortex"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that even when a plant is infected with a virus, its meristem (apical and axillary) is free of virus.\nMeristem cultured in vitro therefore gives virus-free plants. This has been done for banana, sugarcane and potato.\nPhloem is a tempting choice, but it is a main route by which viruses spread, so it is a poor explant.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about somatic hybridisation is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Protoplasts released after wall digestion are surrounded only by the plasma membrane","Protoplasts from two different varieties, each having a desirable character, can be fused","The fused hybrid protoplast can be grown further to form a new plant","The plant obtained is called a somaclone because it is genetically identical to one of the parents"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: digesting the wall with cellulase and pectinase leaves naked protoplasts bounded by the plasma membrane.\n(2) Correct: NCERT describes fusing protoplasts of two varieties that each carry a desirable trait.\n(3) Correct: the hybrid protoplast regenerates into a new plant.\n(4) Incorrect: this plant is a somatic hybrid carrying genetic material from both parents. 'Somaclone' refers to identical copies produced by micropropagation.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"To obtain naked protoplasts from plant cells, the cell walls are digested using:","image":"","options":["Cellulase and pectinase","Chitinase and lysozyme","Lipase and protease","Amylase and pectinase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The plant cell wall is made mainly of cellulose. Pectin binds adjacent cells together.\nCellulase digests cellulose and pectinase digests pectin.\nTogether they release protoplasts bounded only by the plasma membrane.\nChitinase acts on fungal walls and lysozyme acts on bacterial walls, so they are not suitable.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (aspect of dairy farm management) with List-II (what NCERT emphasises about it).\nList-I:\nA. Selection of breed\nB. Feeding\nC. Cleanliness and hygiene\nD. Regular inspection with record keeping\nList-II:\n(i) Helps identify and rectify problems as early as possible\n(ii) Scientific manner, with emphasis on quality and quantity of fodder\n(iii) High yield potential combined with resistance to diseases\n(iv) Of paramount importance during milking, storage and transport of milk","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): good breeds should combine high yield potential with resistance to diseases.\nB-(ii): feeding must be scientific, paying attention to both quality and quantity of fodder.\nC-(iv): hygiene of cattle and handlers is paramount during milking, storage and transport.\nD-(i): regular inspections with records help detect and correct problems early.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which species of honeybee is the most commonly reared in Indian apiculture?","image":"","options":["$Apis\\ indica$","$Apis\\ florea$","$Apis\\ dorsata$","$Bombyx\\ mori$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that several species of honeybees can be reared, and the most common is $Apis\\ indica$.\n$Apis\\ florea$ and $Apis\\ dorsata$ are honeybees, but NCERT does not name them as the commonly reared species.\n$Bombyx\\ mori$ is the silkworm, not a honeybee, so it is the easiest option to rule out.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Parbhani Kranti, a variety developed by transferring a resistance gene from a wild species, belongs to which crop and resists which disease?","image":"","options":["Mung bean; powdery mildew","Chilli; leaf curl","Bhindi (Abelmoschus esculentus); yellow mosaic virus","Cowpea; bacterial blight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A gene for resistance to yellow mosaic virus was transferred from a wild species into bhindi (Abelmoschus esculentus).\nThis produced the variety Parbhani Kranti.\nMung bean also gained resistance to yellow mosaic virus and powdery mildew, but through induced mutation, not through a wild relative.\nThe chilli and cowpea options belong to other Pusa varieties.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following steps of MOET in the correct sequence:\nP. Transfer of embryos to surrogate mothers\nQ. Administration of hormone with FSH-like activity to the donor cow\nR. Mating with an elite bull or artificial insemination\nS. Non-surgical recovery of 8-32 cell stage embryos\nT. Superovulation, releasing 6-8 eggs","image":"","options":["Q → T → R → S → P","T → Q → R → P → S","Q → R → T → S → P","R → Q → T → S → P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: the hormone with FSH-like activity is given first (Q).\nStep 2: this causes follicle maturation and superovulation of 6-8 eggs (T).\nStep 3: the cow is mated with an elite bull or artificially inseminated (R).\nStep 4: embryos at the 8-32 cell stage are recovered non-surgically (S).\nStep 5: the embryos are transferred to surrogate mothers (P).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The semi-dwarf rice varieties Jaya and Ratna were developed at IRRI, Philippines, and later introduced into India.\nStatement II: Saccharum officinarum, grown in South India, has thicker stems and higher sugar content but does not grow well in North India.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The semi-dwarf parents IR-8 (from IRRI, Philippines) and Taichung Native-1 (from Taiwan) came from abroad. However, Jaya and Ratna were developed in India.\nStatement II is correct. S. officinarum has thick stems and high sugar but grows poorly in the north. North Indian S. barberi had poor sugar content and yield, and the two were crossed to combine their traits.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"The term 'Blue Revolution', described as being implemented along the same lines as the Green Revolution, refers to increased production in:","image":"","options":["Milk and dairy products","Fisheries and aquatic food products","Poultry and eggs","Oilseed crops"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aquaculture and pisciculture raised the production of aquatic plants and animals, and the fishery industry flourished as a result.\nNCERT calls this growth in fisheries the 'Blue Revolution', modelled on the Green Revolution in cereals.\nThe tempting wrong option, milk production, is linked to the White Revolution, not the Blue Revolution.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"An elite potato variety has become infected with a virus. A grower wants a large number of plants that are free of the virus and genetically identical to this variety. Which approach is most suitable?","image":"","options":["Fuse its leaf protoplasts with those of a virus-resistant wild species and raise the somatic hybrids","Excise apical or axillary meristems, culture them in vitro and micropropagate the resulting plantlets","Culture mature leaf mesophyll cells of the infected plant and regenerate plants by micropropagation","Cross-pollinate it with a healthy variety and raise plants from the true seeds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Two goals must be met: the plants must be virus-free and genetically identical to the variety.\nMeristems are free of virus even in infected plants. Culturing them and micropropagating the plantlets gives virus-free somaclones, which meets both goals.\nLeaf cells carry the virus, so option (3) fails.\nSomatic hybrids and seedlings from a cross mix two genomes, so they are not identical to the variety. Options (1) and (4) fail.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Sonalika\n(b) IR-8\n(c) Taichung Native-1\n(d) Saccharum officinarum\nList-II\n(i) Semi-dwarf rice parent developed in Taiwan\n(ii) Thick stem and high sugar content, but grows poorly in north India\n(iii) Semi-dwarf wheat variety\n(iv) Semi-dwarf rice developed at IRRI, Philippines","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Sonalika is a semi-dwarf wheat variety, so a-iii.\n(b) IR-8 was developed at IRRI in the Philippines, so b-iv.\n(c) Taichung Native-1 came from Taiwan, so c-i.\n(d) Saccharum officinarum is the tropical south Indian cane with thick stems and high sugar, which grows poorly in north India, so d-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following steps of a plant breeding programme in the correct order:\n(A) Selection and testing of superior recombinants\n(B) Evaluation and selection of parents\n(C) Testing, release and commercialisation of new cultivars\n(D) Collection of variability\n(E) Cross hybridisation among the selected parents","image":"","options":["D → B → E → A → C","B → D → E → A → C","D → E → B → A → C","D → B → A → E → C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Collect variability (germplasm), D.\nStep 2: Evaluate the germplasm and select parents with desirable characters, B.\nStep 3: Cross the selected parents to combine those characters, E.\nStep 4: Select and test the superior recombinants from the progeny, A.\nStep 5: Test, release and commercialise the new cultivar, C.\nOption (4) is wrong because recombinants cannot be selected before the cross is made.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Bird flu, which led to the large-scale killing of poultry in several Indian states, is caused by:","image":"","options":["The bacterium Salmonella","The H5N1 strain of avian influenza virus","A protozoan parasite","A fungus infecting the feathers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Bird flu (avian influenza) is a viral disease.\nThe strain responsible for the outbreaks is H5N1, an influenza virus.\nSalmonella is a bacterium. It can contaminate poultry products, but it does not cause bird flu.\nProtozoa and fungi are not the cause of bird flu.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plant diseases are caused by fungi?\nBrown rust of wheat, Red rot of sugarcane, Late blight of potato, Black rot of crucifers, Tobacco mosaic, Turnip mosaic","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fungal (as NCERT classifies them): brown rust of wheat, red rot of sugarcane and late blight of potato. That makes three.\nBacterial: black rot of crucifers.\nViral: tobacco mosaic and turnip mosaic.\nSo exactly three of the six diseases are fungal.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (crop variety) with List-II (disease it resists) and choose the correct option.\nList-I\nA. Himgiri (wheat)\nB. Pusa Swarnim (Brassica)\nC. Pusa Shubhra (cauliflower)\nD. Pusa Sadabahar (chilli)\nList-II\n(i) White rust\n(ii) Chilly mosaic virus, tobacco mosaic virus and leaf curl\n(iii) Leaf and stripe rust, hill bunt\n(iv) Black rot and curl blight black rot","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A: Himgiri wheat resists leaf and stripe rust and hill bunt, so A matches (iii).\nB: Pusa Swarnim (Karan rai) resists white rust, so B matches (i).\nC: Pusa Shubhra cauliflower resists black rot and curl blight black rot, so C matches (iv).\nD: Pusa Sadabahar chilli resists chilly mosaic virus, TMV and leaf curl, so D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A farmer grows three crops. His mustard is badly attacked by aphids. His flat bean suffers from jassids and fruit borer. His okra is damaged by shoot and fruit borer. Which set of NCERT-listed resistant varieties should he choose for mustard, flat bean and okra, respectively?","image":"","options":["Pusa Gaurav, Pusa Sem 2, Pusa Sawani","Pusa Swarnim, Pusa Sem 3, Pusa A-4","Pusa Gaurav, Pusa Sawani, Pusa Sem 2","Pusa Sem 2, Pusa Gaurav, Pusa A-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mustard (Brassica) with aphids needs Pusa Gaurav. Pusa Swarnim is a Brassica variety resistant to white rust, which is a disease, not a pest.\nFlat bean with jassids and fruit borer needs Pusa Sem 2 or Pusa Sem 3.\nOkra with shoot and fruit borer needs Pusa Sawani or Pusa A-4.\nOption (3) puts the okra variety on bean and the bean variety on okra. Option (4) also mixes up the crops.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Fishery includes the catching, processing and selling of fish, shellfish and other aquatic animals. Which one of the following aquatic food animals is NOT a fish?","image":"","options":["Rohu","Pomfret","Lobster","Mackerel"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rohu is a freshwater fish.\nPomfret and mackerel are marine fishes.\nThe lobster is a crustacean (shellfish). Like prawn and crab, it is an aquatic food animal but not a true fish.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Inbreeding helps in the elimination of less desirable genes from a breeding population.\nReason (R): Inbreeding increases homozygosity, so harmful recessive genes become exposed and can be removed by selection.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that inbreeding helps accumulate superior genes and eliminate less desirable genes.\nR is true. Inbreeding increases homozygosity, so recessive alleles appear in the homozygous state and express themselves.\nOnce harmful recessive genes are expressed, the affected animals can be identified and removed by selection.\nThis is exactly how undesirable genes get eliminated, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about bird flu and poultry is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The H5N1 virus can infect humans as well as poultry","Increased movement of people and birds across regions can help such infections spread faster","Eating properly cooked chicken and eggs is the main route by which humans get bird flu","Maintaining hygiene and health care on poultry farms helps reduce the risk of outbreaks"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: H5N1 infects poultry and can also infect humans.\n(2) Correct: globalisation and travel speed up the spread of disease.\n(3) Incorrect: cooking above 100°C inactivates the virus, so properly cooked chicken and eggs carry negligible risk. Infection is linked mainly to contact with infected birds.\n(4) Correct: hygiene and health care are key parts of farm management.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the birds that typically make up 'poultry' are:","image":"","options":["Chicken and ducks, and sometimes turkey and geese","Only chicken","Pigeons, parrots and peacocks","Ostriches and emus only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines poultry as domesticated fowl raised for food or eggs.\nThe birds typically included are chicken and ducks, and sometimes turkey and geese.\nOption (2) is too narrow because ducks are also typical poultry.\nOptions (3) and (4) name birds that are not described as poultry in NCERT.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In the Multiple Ovulation Embryo Transfer (MOET) programme, a cow is given a hormone to induce follicular maturation and superovulation. Which hormone activity is this treatment based on?","image":"","options":["LH-like activity","FSH-like activity","Oxytocin-like activity","Prolactin-like activity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In MOET, the cow receives hormones with FSH-like activity.\nFSH (follicle stimulating hormone) stimulates follicles to mature, so many ova are released together. This is superovulation.\nLH triggers ovulation of a mature follicle, but NCERT names FSH-like activity for MOET.\nOxytocin and prolactin act in parturition and lactation, not follicle maturation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An iron-fortified rice variety has been developed that contains over five times as much iron as commonly consumed varieties.\nStatement II: Bathua was released by IARI as a vitamin A enriched vegetable.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that the iron-fortified rice contains over five times the iron of common varieties.\nStatement II is incorrect. Bathua is listed under vitamin C enriched and under iron and calcium enriched vegetables, not vitamin A.\nThe vitamin A enriched vegetables are carrot, spinach and pumpkin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Smooth-leaved and nectar-less varieties of cotton do not attract bollworms.\nStatement II: A high sugar and high nitrogen content in maize makes it resistant to maize stem borers.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT gives smooth-leaved, nectar-less cotton as a morphological basis of resistance to bollworms.\nStatement II is incorrect. Resistance of maize to stem borers is linked to a high aspartic acid content and a low nitrogen and sugar content, not a high one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"The term 'hidden hunger' refers to:","image":"","options":["Hunger felt by people who skip meals because of fasting","Lack of enough calories in the diet of over 840 million people","Deficiency of micronutrients, proteins and vitamins in the diet, even when people may not be starving","Hunger caused by intestinal parasites that absorb the host's food"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT uses 'hidden hunger' for deficiencies of micronutrients, protein and vitamins.\nAbout three billion people are affected because they cannot afford enough fruits, vegetables, legumes, fish and meat.\nThe figure of over 840 million refers to a different problem: people who lack adequate food altogether.\nOption 2 is tempting for that reason, but hidden hunger is about poor diet quality, not too little food.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Before a new crop variety is released, it is tested in farmers' fields at several locations representing all the agroclimatic zones of the country. For a minimum of how many growing seasons is this testing done?","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The first evaluation of a new line is done in research fields.\nThe line is then tested in farmers' fields for at least three growing seasons, at several locations covering all agroclimatic zones where the crop is usually grown.\nOne or two seasons would not reveal how the line performs across varying conditions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Between 1960 and 2000, wheat production in India rose from 11 million tonnes to 75 million tonnes, while rice production rose from 35 million tonnes to 89.5 million tonnes. So the proportional increase was much greater for wheat than for rice.\nStatement II: Sonalika and Kalyan Sona were semi-dwarf varieties developed at IRRI, Philippines, from the rice material of Taichung Native-1.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: wheat rose 75/11, about a 6.8-fold increase. Rice rose 89.5/35, about a 2.6-fold increase. So the proportional rise was much larger for wheat, and the statement is correct.\nStatement II: Sonalika and Kalyan Sona are wheat varieties. They came from the semi-dwarf wheat developed by Borlaug in Mexico and were introduced in 1963. Taichung Native-1 is a Taiwanese rice variety and is unrelated to them, so the statement is incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed by NCERT as important components of poultry farm management?\n(i) Selection of disease-free and suitable breeds\n(ii) Proper and safe farm conditions\n(iii) Proper feed and water\n(iv) Hygiene and health care\n(v) Keeping birds in overcrowded, closed sheds to save space","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: disease-free, suitable breeds are a listed component.\n(ii) Correct: proper and safe farm conditions are listed.\n(iii) Correct: proper feed and water are listed.\n(iv) Correct: hygiene and health care are listed.\n(v) Incorrect: overcrowded, closed sheds promote disease and go against safe farm conditions.\nSo four of the five are components.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following does NCERT describe Spirulina biomass as being rich in?\nProteins, Minerals, Fats, Carbohydrates, Vitamins, Lignin","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT says Spirulina can serve as food rich in protein, minerals, fats, carbohydrates and vitamins.\nThat gives 5 correct items.\nLignin is a component of plant secondary cell walls. It is not a nutrient that Spirulina is noted for, so it is excluded.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Virus-free plants can be obtained from a virus-infected plant by culturing its apical or axillary meristem in vitro.\nReason (R): In a virus-infected plant, the apical and axillary meristems remain free of virus.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT describes recovering healthy plants from diseased ones through meristem culture. This has been done in banana, sugarcane and potato.\nR is true: in an infected plant, the meristems are free of virus.\nR explains A: plants regenerated from virus-free meristem tissue are themselves free of virus.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which morphological feature of wheat gives it resistance against the stem sawfly?","image":"","options":["Hairy leaves","Solid stems","Nectar-less flowers","High aspartic acid content"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Morphological traits can protect plants from insect pests.\nIn wheat, solid stems resist the stem sawfly.\nHairy leaves are also an NCERT example of a resistance trait. They protect cotton against jassids and wheat against the cereal leaf beetle, not against the stem sawfly.\nNectar-less cotton resists bollworms. High aspartic acid in maize is a biochemical trait against stem borers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assume India and China together hold exactly 70% of the world's livestock and contribute 25% of world farm produce from livestock. The rest of the world holds the remaining livestock and produce. Roughly how many times greater is the average produce per animal in the rest of the world than in India and China?","image":"","options":["About 2 times","About 3.5 times","About 7 times","About 10 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"India and China produce 25% from 70% of the animals, so produce per unit share = 25/70 ≈ 0.36.\nThe rest of the world produces 75% from 30% of the animals, so produce per unit share = 75/30 = 2.5.\nRatio = 2.5 / 0.36 ≈ 7.\nThis is why NCERT calls productivity per unit 'very low' in these countries and calls for better breeding technologies.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Breeders crossed Saccharum barberi with Saccharum officinarum to obtain sugarcane varieties suitable for north India.\nReason (R): Saccharum barberi, native to north India, has thicker stems and higher sugar content than Saccharum officinarum.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The cross gave varieties with high yield, thick stems and high sugar content that could also grow in the sugarcane areas of north India.\nR is false. Saccharum barberi of north India has poor sugar content and yield.\nIt is the tropical Saccharum officinarum of south India that has thicker stems and higher sugar. However, it did not grow well in north India.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In cotton, hairy leaves give the plant resistance against which insect pest?","image":"","options":["Bollworms","Jassids","Aphids","Stem borers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists hairy leaves as a morphological trait that protects plants from insect pests.\nIn cotton, hairy leaves give resistance to jassids. In wheat, they give resistance to the cereal leaf beetle.\nBollworms are kept away by a different trait: smooth-leaved, nectar-less cotton.\nAphids and stem borers are linked to other crops (Brassica and maize).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Growing Spirulina on sewage and animal manure helps reduce environmental pollution.\nReason (R): Spirulina uses such waste materials as substrates for growth and converts them into useful protein-rich biomass.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that using wastes such as sewage and manure to grow Spirulina also reduces environmental pollution.\nR is true. Spirulina grows on these wastes and turns them into biomass rich in protein, minerals, fats, carbohydrates and vitamins.\nThe wastes are used up as substrate, and that is exactly why pollution falls. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plant character) with List-II (insect pest it resists) and choose the correct option.\nList-I\nA. Hairy leaves in cotton\nB. Solid stems in wheat\nC. Smooth-leaved, nectar-less cotton\nD. High aspartic acid, low nitrogen and low sugar in maize\nList-II\n(i) Stem sawfly\n(ii) Jassids\n(iii) Maize stem borers\n(iv) Bollworms","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: Hairy leaves in cotton resist jassids, so A matches (ii).\nB: Solid stems in wheat resist the stem sawfly, so B matches (i).\nC: Smooth-leaved, nectar-less cotton does not attract bollworms, so C matches (iv).\nD: In maize, high aspartic acid with low nitrogen and low sugar gives resistance to maize stem borers, so D matches (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"The semi-dwarf wheat varieties Sonalika and Kalyan Sona, introduced across India's wheat belt in 1963, were developed from material bred by Norman E. Borlaug at:","image":"","options":["International Rice Research Institute, Philippines","International Centre for Wheat and Maize Improvement, Mexico","Taichung, Taiwan","Indian Agricultural Research Institute, New Delhi"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that semi-dwarf wheat was developed by Norman E. Borlaug at the International Centre for Wheat and Maize Improvement in Mexico.\nSonalika and Kalyan Sona were high-yielding, disease-resistant varieties derived from this material.\nIRRI (Philippines) and Taiwan are linked with the semi-dwarf rice parents IR-8 and Taichung Native-1, not with wheat.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hisardale sheep and the mule are both products of interspecific hybridisation.\nStatement II: Hybrid progeny obtained by cross-breeding may be subjected to some form of inbreeding and selection to develop new stable breeds that are superior to existing breeds.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Hisardale comes from two breeds of the same species (Bikaneri ewe × Marino ram), so it is cross-breeding.\nOnly the mule (female horse × male donkey) comes from two related species, so only the mule is an interspecific hybrid.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that cross-bred progeny can be used directly for commercial production.\nAlternatively, the cross-bred progeny can be inbred and selected to develop new stable breeds superior to existing ones.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Animal breeders use techniques such as MOET even though artificial insemination is available.\nReason (R): The success rate of crossing mature males and females is often fairly low, even when artificial insemination is used.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: MOET is a herd improvement programme used alongside AI.\nR is true: NCERT states that the success rate of crossing is often fairly low, even with AI.\nR explains A: other methods such as MOET were adopted because of this low success rate, to improve the chances of producing hybrids.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"In animal breeding, a group of animals that are related by descent and are similar in most characters, such as general appearance, features, size and configuration, is called a:","image":"","options":["Species","Breed","Population","Hybrid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines a breed as a group of animals related by descent and similar in most characters, such as appearance, features, size and configuration.\nA species is a broader category. It can contain many breeds, for example the many cattle breeds within one species.\nA population is simply the individuals living in an area. They need not share descent or similar characters.\nA hybrid is the progeny of a cross between different parents, so it is not a group defined by shared descent.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Jaya and Ratna are:","image":"","options":["Semi-dwarf wheat varieties developed in Mexico","Semi-dwarf rice varieties developed in India","Hybrid bajra varieties resistant to water stress","Sugarcane hybrids suited to north India"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The semi-dwarf rice varieties introduced in 1966 were derived from IR-8 and Taichung Native-1.\nNCERT notes that better-yielding semi-dwarf rice varieties, Jaya and Ratna, were later developed in India.\nThe wheat varieties of the Green Revolution were Sonalika and Kalyan Sona, not Jaya and Ratna.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the Green Revolution in India are correct?\n(i) Wheat production rose from about 11 million tonnes to 75 million tonnes between 1960 and 2000.\n(ii) IR-8 was developed in Taiwan and Taichung Native-1 at IRRI, Philippines.\n(iii) Semi-dwarf rice varieties derived from IR-8 and Taichung Native-1 were introduced in India in 1966.\n(iv) Hybrid maize, jowar and bajra developed in India are high yielding and resistant to water stress.\n(v) M.S. Swaminathan is associated with the development of high-yielding wheat varieties and the Green Revolution in India.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: wheat output rose from 11 to 75 million tonnes.\n(ii) Incorrect: the origins are reversed. IR-8 came from IRRI (Philippines) and Taichung Native-1 from Taiwan.\n(iii) Correct: the derived semi-dwarf rice varieties were introduced in 1966.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT describes these hybrids as high yielding and resistant to water stress.\n(v) Correct: Swaminathan led wheat breeding during the Green Revolution.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is NOT among the points listed in NCERT as important for successful bee-keeping?","image":"","options":["Catching and hiving of swarms","Management of beehives during different seasons","Arranging a large labour force, since bee-keeping is highly labour-intensive","Selection of a suitable location for keeping the beehives"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists five keys to successful bee-keeping:\n- knowledge of the nature and habits of bees\n- selection of a suitable location\n- catching and hiving of swarms\n- seasonal management of hives\n- handling and collection of honey and beeswax\nOptions (1), (2) and (4) all appear in this list.\nNCERT specifically says bee-keeping is NOT labour-intensive, so option (3) is wrong on two counts.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Inbreeding depression in a herd can often be overcome by a single outcross.\nReason (R): Out-crossing is the mating of animals of two different species to obtain hybrids.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that a single outcross often helps to overcome inbreeding depression.\nR is false. Out-crossing mates animals of the same breed that have no common ancestors on either side for 4–6 generations.\nMating animals of different species is interspecific hybridisation (for example, the mule).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs consists of two marine fishes?","image":"","options":["Catla and Rohu","Hilsa and Pomfret","Common carp and Mackerel","Rohu and Sardine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists Catla, Rohu and common carp as common freshwater fishes.\nIt lists Hilsa, sardines, mackerel and pomfrets as common marine fishes.\nOnly option (2) has two marine fishes. Options (3) and (4) each pair a freshwater fish (common carp, Rohu) with a marine one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-FOOD-PRODUCTION-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","type":"mcq","question":"A breeding programme for a high-yielding cow uses Multiple Ovulation Embryo Transfer (MOET). Consider the following statements:\nI. The cow is given hormones with FSH-like activity to induce follicular maturation and super ovulation, so that 6–8 eggs are produced per cycle instead of one.\nII. Fertilised eggs at the 8–32 cell stage are recovered non-surgically and transferred to surrogate mothers.\nIII. The surrogate mother is the genetic mother of the calf that is born.\nIV. This technology has been demonstrated only in cattle.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and IV only","I and IV only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"I Correct: FSH-like hormones cause super ovulation, giving 6–8 eggs per cycle.\nII Correct: after mating with an elite bull or artificial insemination, embryos at the 8–32 cell stage are recovered non-surgically and placed in surrogate mothers.\nIII Incorrect: the surrogate only carries the pregnancy. The genetic mother is the super-ovulated donor cow.\nIV Incorrect: MOET has been demonstrated in cattle, sheep, rabbits, buffaloes and mares.\nSo only I and II are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology (pre-2023 edition), Ch 9 · Strategies for Enhancement in Food Production","difficulty":"hard"}]});
